#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124262 :question/id #uuid "bf35c502-5a22-5953-9158-b2464d28c09f" :question/math-academy-id "e-3377" :question/problem "Find the directional derivative of the function $g(x, y) =3x^2+4xy$ at the point $A(0,1)$ in the direction towards the point $B(1,4).$" :question/worked-solution "The directional derivative $g'_{\\mathbf u}(a,b)$ of a function $g(x,y)$ at the point $(a,b)$ in the direction of the vector $\\mathbf v$ is given by\n\n$$\ng'_{\\mathbf u}(a,b) = \\nabla g(a,b)\\cdot\\mathbf u\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf u$ is a unit vector in the direction of $\\mathbf v.$\n\nFirst, we find the direction vector and the corresponding unit vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =⟨1,4⟩-⟨0,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,3⟩ \\\\ u & =\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{AB}}{‖\\overset{\\to }{AB}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{3}^{2}}}⟨1,3⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}⟨1,3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the gradient $\\nabla g(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $A(0,1)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇g(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3{x}^{2}+4xy),\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{x}^{2}+4xy)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨6x+4y,\\,4x⟩ \\\\ ∇g(0,1) & =⟨6\\cdot 0+4\\cdot 1,\\,4\\cdot 0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the directional derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}_{u}^{′}(0,1) & =∇g(0,1)\\cdot u \\\\  & =⟨4,0⟩\\cdot ⟨\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}},\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\sqrt{10}}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{10}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124263 :question/id #uuid "dc1e4548-ce72-5618-86f4-b68b46b50550" :question/math-academy-id "e-3381" :question/problem "In what direction does the function $f (x, y) = \\ln (x^2y)$ decrease most rapidly at the point $(2, 1)?$" :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $f(x,y)$ decreases most rapidly in the direction opposite to that of its gradient.\n\nSo, we compute $-\\nabla f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(2,1)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-∇f(x,y) & =-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}y))\\text{ }i-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}y))\\text{ }j \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}y}\\cdot 2xy\\text{ }i-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}y}\\cdot {x}^{2}j \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{x}\\text{ }i-\\frac{1}{y}\\text{ }j \\\\ -∇f(2,1) & =-\\frac{2}{2}\\text{ }i-\\frac{1}{1}\\text{ }j \\\\  & =-i-j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function decreases most rapidly in the direction of $- \\, \\mathbf i - \\mathbf j.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124264 :question/id #uuid "1eea4610-508a-5437-802e-9d009b777e82" :question/math-academy-id "e-3382" :question/problem "Consider the function $g (x, y) = \\ln \\left(\\sqrt {xy^3}\\right).$ What is the rate of change of $g$ at the point $\\left(\\dfrac 12, \\dfrac 32 \\right)$ in the direction in which the function increases most rapidly?" :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $g(x,y)$ increases most rapidly in the direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nIn our case, we compute $\\nabla g(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $$\n\\displaystyle \\left(\\dfrac 12, \\dfrac 32 \\right)\\!\\mathbin{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇g(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{x{y}^{3}})),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{x{y}^{3}}))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x{y}^{3}}}\\cdot \\frac{{y}^{3}}{2\\sqrt{x{y}^{3}}},\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x{y}^{3}}}\\cdot \\frac{3x{y}^{2}}{2\\sqrt{x{y}^{3}}}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{2x},\\,\\frac{3}{2y}⟩ \\\\ ∇g(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{3}{2}) & =⟨\\frac{1}{2\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)},\\frac{3}{2\\cdot \\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the rate of change of $g(x,y)$ at the point $\\left(\\dfrac 12, \\dfrac 32 \\right)$ in the direction of $$\n\\displaystyle \\nabla g\\left(\\dfrac 12, \\dfrac 32 \\right)\n$$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖∇g(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{3}{2})‖ & =‖⟨1,1⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124962 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "91e3020e-b220-554e-a3f0-07e483342e90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4\\sqrt{21}}{21}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122373} {:answer/id #uuid "a1e6986e-ec79-5752-9e0d-ea5960beab16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14\\sqrt{29}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122374} {:answer/id #uuid "a8340c19-e527-5c1b-953c-c260766408b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{14\\sqrt{21}}{21}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122375} {:answer/id #uuid "db0760d0-b134-5ae3-af68-54c39c2f1781" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{10\\sqrt{29}}{29}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122376} {:answer/id #uuid "0801896c-1c2b-510b-86df-c0542a7bf4e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{14\\sqrt{29}}{29}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122377}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0801896c-1c2b-510b-86df-c0542a7bf4e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{14\\sqrt{29}}{29}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122377} :answer-field/id #uuid "5dfb4078-c321-5580-bb20-9356ac667745" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122372}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "4ba373e3-c7d5-54ce-a51c-b2950c874b69" :question/math-academy-id "q-36273" :question/problem "Find the directional derivative of the function $f(x,y)=2x+{y}^{2}$ at the point $(0,1)$ in the direction of the vector $v=5i+2j.$" :question/worked-solution "The directional derivative ${f}_{u}^{′}(a,b)$ of a function $f(x,y)$ at the point $(a,b)$ in the direction of the vector $v$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{u}^{′}(a,b)=∇f(a,b)\\cdot u\n$$\n\nwhere $u$ is a unit vector in the direction of $v.$\n\nFirst, we find the unit vector $u$ in the direction of $v:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖ & =\\sqrt{{5}^{2}+{2}^{2}}=\\sqrt{29} \\\\ u & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{29}}(5i+2j)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the gradient $∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(0,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x+{y}^{2})\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x+{y}^{2})\\text{ }j \\\\  & =2\\,i+2y\\,j \\\\ ∇\\,f(0,1) & =2\\,i+(2\\cdot 1)\\,j \\\\  & =2i+2j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the directional derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{u}^{′}(0,1) & =∇\\,f(0,1)\\cdot u \\\\  & =(2i+2j)\\cdot (\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{29}}i+\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{29}}j) \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot 5+2\\cdot 2}{\\sqrt{29}} \\\\  & =\\frac{14\\sqrt{29}}{29}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124963 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "476511f4-e638-543b-8280-a5b7db9ba896" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1)-2)\\,i-j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446190} {:answer/id #uuid "5ebdb20c-42b9-5f29-a21e-e6adefd06cd3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1)\\,i+2j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446199} {:answer/id #uuid "6d736c41-9b37-5824-a068-b4c8dfbf00b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2i+j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446203} {:answer/id #uuid "7d2a5ab5-1e1e-50a2-abb5-df9207b0da19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1))\\,i+j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446206} {:answer/id #uuid "b601e5c8-a43b-53e7-ba27-d8c6515bae90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2i-j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446219}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7d2a5ab5-1e1e-50a2-abb5-df9207b0da19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1))\\,i+j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446206} :answer-field/id #uuid "0e3d8181-5a0a-57e3-b64b-0cfbd5cc5890" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122378}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e75dd59c-9b9f-50d9-a9a4-c1850c66ee84" :question/math-academy-id "q-36293" :question/problem "In what direction does the function $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+{x}^{2}y$ increase most rapidly at the point $(1,1)?$" :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $f(x,y)$ increases most rapidly in the direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nSo, we compute $∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+{x}^{2}y)\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+{x}^{2}y)\\text{ }j \\\\  & =(2xy-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\text{ }i+{x}^{2}\\text{ }j \\\\ ∇f(1,1) & =(2\\cdot 1\\cdot 1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1))\\text{ }i+{1}^{2}\\text{ }j \\\\  & =(2-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1))\\text{ }i+j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function increases most rapidly in the direction of $(2-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1))\\,i+j.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124964 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8377f097-45c2-5136-bb1a-ec94780f46ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122385} {:answer/id #uuid "85e45877-a3bb-5df2-b95b-1b22f8f282b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122386} {:answer/id #uuid "c53a2aeb-cf40-56b1-837e-e4a412f6db98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "17" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122387} {:answer/id #uuid "42f3f813-ee54-55f9-9e98-8cd208a09b33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{8\\sqrt{10}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122388} {:answer/id #uuid "4a7e03b0-95c8-5010-8940-20600f4e7159" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122389}] :answer-field/id #uuid "ff58ae13-957c-59b0-9fda-ff457d0f11e3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122384}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a985e88b-265b-5691-956a-877ec947cdff" :question/math-academy-id "q-36297" :question/problem "Consider the function $g(x, y) = \\frac{x^{2}}{y}$. What is the rate of change of $g(x, y)$ at the point $(2, 1)$ in the direction of the vector $v = ⟨ - 1, 3⟩$?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124965 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08ff85bb-8ce2-5025-a0a1-83fc98417ed7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-2\\pi ,-2,-\\pi ⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321217} {:answer/id #uuid "636513d9-9ad2-5bbe-b8b3-cd67a4bf8d3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨2,2,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321920} {:answer/id #uuid "9f98fa5a-9408-52f3-8b7e-8f25e3ab2ecf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-\\pi ,-1,\\pi ⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322407} {:answer/id #uuid "c672059e-d68f-556c-9a70-117d11270e16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\pi ,2,\\pi ⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322686} {:answer/id #uuid "cdeb1fca-19f0-5446-8266-9a4f0c5b2778" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨2\\pi ,2,\\pi ⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322752}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cdeb1fca-19f0-5446-8266-9a4f0c5b2778" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨2\\pi ,2,\\pi ⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322752} :answer-field/id #uuid "cd3b7500-df79-56f9-843a-ede4ff70a4be" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323570}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "9a412c3a-6b92-5f8b-8cf9-ec76f9ca97fa" :question/math-academy-id "q-36305" :question/problem "In what direction does the function $g(x,y,z)={y}^{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi x+\\pi z)$ increase most rapidly at the point $(1,1,2)?$" :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $g(x,y,z)$ increases most rapidly in the direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nSo, we compute $∇g(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at the point $(1,1,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇g(x,y,z) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi x+\\pi z)),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi x+\\pi z)),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂z}({y}^{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi x+\\pi z))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi x+\\pi z),\\,2y,\\,\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi x+\\pi z)⟩ \\\\ ∇g(1,1,2) & =⟨2\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi \\cdot 1+\\pi \\cdot 2),\\,2\\cdot 1,\\,\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi \\cdot 1+\\pi \\cdot 2)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\pi ),\\,2,\\,\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\pi )⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2\\pi ,\\,2,\\,\\pi ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function increases most rapidly in the direction of $⟨2\\pi ,\\,2,\\,\\pi ⟩.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125028 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10d46f12-0b75-50c9-a911-0b663e2a7dd5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{\\pi +e}}{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446179} {:answer/id #uuid "1683b5bd-9e3c-568d-b68d-d3cd665c9d95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{{\\pi }^{4}+{e}^{2}}}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446180} {:answer/id #uuid "5e6ee910-194a-5db1-a61b-cb7e82e92ef2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{{\\pi }^{2}+{e}^{2}}}{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446198} {:answer/id #uuid "7f7aff75-c59d-50b0-ace5-77c397f97e98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{{\\pi }^{2}+{e}^{2}}}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446208} {:answer/id #uuid "cfe40dc8-4b41-5a6b-bd2a-d1a558dc2365" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\frac{\\pi +e}{e}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446224}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5e6ee910-194a-5db1-a61b-cb7e82e92ef2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{{\\pi }^{2}+{e}^{2}}}{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446198} :answer-field/id #uuid "4aca3088-e8f0-5dbc-95a5-523c9e4320d2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122768}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "8ea3ebc5-2c81-5696-aab4-931973a19233" :question/math-academy-id "q-37074" :question/problem "Consider the function $g(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x).$ What is the rate of change of $g(x,y)$ at the point $(e,\\pi )$ in the direction in which the function increases most rapidly?" :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $g(x,y)$ increases most rapidly in the direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nIn our case, we compute $∇g(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(e,\\pi ):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,g(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)\\cdot \\frac{y}{x},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡x⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}{x},\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)⟩ \\\\ ∇\\,g(e,\\pi ) & =⟨\\frac{\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi \\operatorname{ln}⁡e)}{e},\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡e\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi \\operatorname{ln}⁡e)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi }{e},\\,1\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\pi \\cdot (-1)}{e},\\,1\\cdot (-1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{\\pi }{e},\\,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the rate of change of $g(x,y)$ at the point $(e,\\pi )$ in the direction of $∇g(e,\\pi )$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖∇\\,g(e,\\pi )‖ & =‖⟨-\\frac{\\pi }{e},-1⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{{e}^{2}}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{{\\pi }^{2}+{e}^{2}}}{e}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125029 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "93fcdd41-5aba-54fd-9f19-d383cd0c8b1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{78}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122775} {:answer/id #uuid "abdb47d5-6a5e-50f3-b8d6-ae7cb6744ce6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{18}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122776} {:answer/id #uuid "b32de488-c397-50e7-9e0d-a9d2fa222187" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{18}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122777} {:answer/id #uuid "5be9dc56-00c0-5419-8e09-cdc3986dabdf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{21}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122778} {:answer/id #uuid "6f19ba24-1488-5e98-a493-56f081245961" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{78}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122779}] :answer-field/id #uuid "52446f3c-8c33-54d6-a7e9-42d22d6cabce" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122774}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f655da5c-6e4d-590f-afb2-3ac467aa95f7" :question/math-academy-id "q-37077" :question/problem "Find the directional derivative of the function $f(x, y) = (x + y^{2})^{2}$ at the point $A(-1,-2)$ in the direction towards the point $B(2, 2)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235525 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7eaac021-36b6-54cc-b7ec-c212007fad51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234738}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7eaac021-36b6-54cc-b7ec-c212007fad51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234738} :answer-field/id #uuid "61ede809-36cc-5d59-b1d8-f7a0b29d9d5f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235075}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e02c41aa-7da1-51d0-a8bf-4d3360c07bca" :question/math-academy-id "q-168116" :question/problem "Find the directional derivative of the function $f(x,y)=\\text{div}\\,V$ at the point $(1,1)$ in the direction of the vector $v=-3\\,i+4\\,j.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \nThe required directional derivative is ${f}_{u}^{′}(1,1)=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n{{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ." :question/worked-solution "The directional derivative ${f}_{u}^{′}(a,b)$ of a function $f(x,y)$ at the point $(a,b)$ in the direction of the vector $v$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{u}^{′}(a,b)=∇f(a,b)\\cdot u\n$$\n\nwhere $u$ is a unit vector in the direction of $v.$\n\nFirst, we find the unit vector $u$ in the direction of $v:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖ & =\\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+{4}^{2}}=\\sqrt{25}=5 \\\\ u & =\\frac{1}{5}(-3\\,i+4\\,j)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn part 3, we found that the gradient $∇f(x,y)$ at the point $(1,1)$ is\n\n$$\n∇\\,f(1,1)=∇(\\text{div}\\,V)\\,{|}_{(1,\\,1)}=4\\,i+2\\,j.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the directional derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{u}^{′}(1,1) & =∇\\,f(1,1)\\cdot u \\\\  & =(4\\,i+2\\,j)\\cdot (\\frac{1}{5}(-3\\,i+4\\,j)) \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\cdot (-3)+2\\cdot 4}{5} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265867 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08151725-c767-5bfa-8cc0-566dac73c7c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}+2}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263073} {:answer/id #uuid "6b75a71e-ff62-5063-9c04-4318296a44fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263928} {:answer/id #uuid "8f591e1b-9777-5d0f-b497-43f656adfa40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}+4}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264230} {:answer/id #uuid "bd6ad8b4-a673-5008-ae07-fbef7600a1fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264645} {:answer/id #uuid "bd888fe4-7f5e-51b1-a53d-363189aedc9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264646}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "08151725-c767-5bfa-8cc0-566dac73c7c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}+2}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263073} :answer-field/id #uuid "93ddfe34-8e2a-5c0d-ad1d-5d50a7d04569" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265512}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "5e9cc9e7-1d62-51d1-8fa2-dd51fa7daa28" :question/math-academy-id "q-130257" :question/problem "Find the directional derivative of the function $f(x,y)=xy$ at the point $(2,1)$ in the direction of the vector $v=\\sqrt{3}\\,i+j.$" :question/worked-solution "The directional derivative ${f}_{u}^{′}(a,b)$ of a function $f(x,y)$ at the point $(a,b)$ in the direction of the vector $v$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{u}^{′}(a,b)=∇f(a,b)\\cdot u\n$$\n\nwhere $u$ is a unit vector in the direction of $v.$\n\nFirst, we find the unit vector $u$ in the direction of $v:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖ & =\\sqrt{(\\sqrt{3})^{2}+{1}^{2}}=2 \\\\ u & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\sqrt{3}\\,i+j)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the gradient $∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(2,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy)\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy)\\text{ }j \\\\  & =y\\,i+x\\,j \\\\ ∇\\,f(2,1) & =i+2\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the directional derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{u}^{′}(2,1) & =∇\\,f(2,1)\\cdot u \\\\  & =(i+2\\,j)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}(\\sqrt{3}\\,i+j) \\\\  & =\\frac{1(\\sqrt{3})+2(1)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}+2}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265868 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1da1714c-2291-5808-ac0e-0808ab462f8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263272} {:answer/id #uuid "450b63f8-2ed8-5c35-8a78-b5c67477b5ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263607} {:answer/id #uuid "72992976-c623-506b-9f6f-3a6145bc7b3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{8}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263992} {:answer/id #uuid "7dc54a79-4160-5b82-ac56-cae08fe005df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264084} {:answer/id #uuid "b7690e09-485b-5e3c-b1cf-05000b42442a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264589}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "72992976-c623-506b-9f6f-3a6145bc7b3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{8}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263992} :answer-field/id #uuid "7c8b53a4-366c-5e74-a168-e6f6544ed5ee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265461}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "35885778-6551-5cbf-9f14-c659423ae334" :question/math-academy-id "q-130260" :question/problem "Find the directional derivative of the function $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})$ at the point $(0,-1)$ in the direction of the vector $v=3\\,i+4\\,j.$" :question/worked-solution "The directional derivative ${f}_{u}^{′}(a,b)$ of a function $f(x,y)$ at the point $(a,b)$ in the direction of the vector $v$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{u}^{′}(a,b)=∇f(a,b)\\cdot u\n$$\n\nwhere $u$ is a unit vector in the direction of $v.$\n\nFirst, we find the unit vector $u$ in the direction of $v:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖ & =\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{4}^{2}}=5 \\\\ u & =\\frac{1}{5}(3\\,i+4\\,j)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the gradient $∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(0,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}))\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}))\\text{ }j \\\\  & =\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,i+\\frac{2y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}j \\\\ ∇\\,f(0,-1) & =\\frac{2(0)}{{0}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}\\,i+\\frac{2(-1)}{{0}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}\\,j \\\\  & =-2\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the directional derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{u}^{′}(0,-1) & =∇\\,f(0,-1)\\cdot u \\\\  & =-2\\,j\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}(3\\,i+4\\,j) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2(4)}{5} \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265869 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "086fecd5-a126-5f32-9308-1bc895c050b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263080} {:answer/id #uuid "8d43de9a-94e9-5878-939e-2733944f61c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264208} {:answer/id #uuid "9b78ab4c-27f6-5a87-8918-b6215e38243b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264329} {:answer/id #uuid "b92952ce-92d6-5fe1-84dc-4e5ec973c535" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264605} {:answer/id #uuid "cd1aafca-14b7-5d95-9816-988f20570192" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264795}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9b78ab4c-27f6-5a87-8918-b6215e38243b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264329} :answer-field/id #uuid "6e1d9c83-156f-56e6-8ce6-3d01bf086064" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265433}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ae068a0a-3a23-5516-a4b7-13c70903e5db" :question/math-academy-id "q-130263" :question/problem "Find the directional derivative of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-xy$ at the point $(1,1)$ in the direction of the vector $v=i-j.$" :question/worked-solution "The directional derivative ${f}_{u}^{′}(a,b)$ of a function $f(x,y)$ at the point $(a,b)$ in the direction of the vector $v$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{u}^{′}(a,b)=∇f(a,b)\\cdot u\n$$\n\nwhere $u$ is a unit vector in the direction of $v.$\n\nFirst, we find the unit vector $u$ in the direction of $v:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖ & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}=\\sqrt{2} \\\\ u & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(i-j)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the gradient $∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-xy)i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-xy)j \\\\  & =(2x-y)\\,i+(2y-x)\\,j \\\\ ∇\\,f(1,1) & =(2(1)-1)\\,i+(2(1)-1)\\,j \\\\  & =i+j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the directional derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{u}^{′}(1,1) & =∇\\,f(1,1)\\cdot u \\\\  & =(i+j)\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(i-j) \\\\  & =\\frac{1(1)+1(-1)}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265872 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "371188b1-b7ec-57b7-b295-164f4505e38f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i-2\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263494} {:answer/id #uuid "468d5107-6a48-54fc-a274-623f490c7ae3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,i+j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263617} {:answer/id #uuid "5224940d-41f5-5791-9620-631ff60b6036" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7\\,i+10\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263707} {:answer/id #uuid "5a9a260e-6d34-52c4-a50b-774f0edf8d5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\,i+j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263781} {:answer/id #uuid "c1f338bb-07ad-5dd5-a554-45630ad32b40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,i-5\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264684}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5224940d-41f5-5791-9620-631ff60b6036" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7\\,i+10\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263707} :answer-field/id #uuid "82b1bccb-f245-5952-bbe0-74fd47a6963c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265475}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "234c72df-7b34-5698-b613-f1acebf8fd8e" :question/math-academy-id "q-130276" :question/problem "In what direction does the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+3xy+2{y}^{2}$ increase most rapidly at the point $(2,1)?$" :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $f(x,y)$ increases most rapidly in the direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nSo, we compute $∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(2,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+3xy+2{y}^{2})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+3xy+2{y}^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =(2x+3y)\\,i+(3x+4y)\\,j \\\\ ∇f(2,1) & =(2(2)+3(1))\\,i+(3(2)+4(1))\\,j \\\\  & =7\\,i+10\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function increases most rapidly in the direction of $7\\,i+10\\,j.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265978 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a5cc75b-1aa1-5b30-9362-5f8752961da4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263250} {:answer/id #uuid "1d98f894-779c-5822-84b3-85b9a53ad6b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263271} {:answer/id #uuid "4efb98a8-8a82-5879-9aee-9a09d6afe439" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263687} {:answer/id #uuid "bcb30fc7-6c64-5f56-9344-f70ad5eab684" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264636} {:answer/id #uuid "e5a55fec-f5cc-59e3-8bae-75fcaf8ea6a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265001}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e5a55fec-f5cc-59e3-8bae-75fcaf8ea6a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265001} :answer-field/id #uuid "f0ef6581-d8af-5b79-a84d-4f8dc281dbef" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265678}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "129e769a-9e81-5bb8-a357-71dd7d678811" :question/math-academy-id "q-130801" :question/problem "Consider the function $g(x,y)=\\frac{x}{y}+\\frac{y}{x}.$ What is the rate of change of $g(x,y)$ at the point $(-1,1)$ in the direction of the vector $v=⟨1,-\\sqrt{2}⟩?$" :question/worked-solution "The rate of change of $g(x,y)$ at the point $(-1,1)$ in the direction of $v$ is equal to the directional derivative ${g}_{u}^{′}(-1,1).$ \n\nFirst, we find the unit vector $u$ in the direction of $v:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖ & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-\\sqrt{2})^{2}}=\\sqrt{3} \\\\ u & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨1,-\\sqrt{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the gradient $∇g(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(-1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇g(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{x}{y}+\\frac{y}{x}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{x}{y}+\\frac{y}{x})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{y}-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}},\\,\\frac{1}{x}-\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}⟩ \\\\ ∇g(-1,1) & =⟨\\frac{1}{1}-\\frac{1}{(-1)^{2}},\\frac{1}{(-1)}-\\frac{(-1)}{{1}^{2}}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the directional derivative ${g}_{u}^{′}(-1,1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}_{u}^{′}(-1,1) & =∇g(-1,1)\\cdot u \\\\  & =⟨0,0⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨1,-\\sqrt{2}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{0(1)+0(-\\sqrt{2})}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323790 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00835dd1-8061-59e4-8040-48a957847175" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321154} {:answer/id #uuid "2b84de9a-1d96-5d76-b304-fcc568ae08c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321481} {:answer/id #uuid "8f8d9dd6-ab7c-531c-92df-429bb88b6255" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322286} {:answer/id #uuid "93873ce7-adfb-5826-85f5-744afae73ad2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322319} {:answer/id #uuid "f4e6066c-22d2-5127-a385-fb4a7da7ae82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323067}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f4e6066c-22d2-5127-a385-fb4a7da7ae82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323067} :answer-field/id #uuid "e2ab9660-72ee-5cec-b767-3be5f30759da" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323617}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "0cd131d3-dbf5-532a-a1c1-f69d113636a0" :question/math-academy-id "q-36290" :question/problem "Find the directional derivative of the function $f(x,y)=x{e}^{y}+{x}^{2}y$ at the point $A(1,0)$ in the direction towards the point $B(0,1).$" :question/worked-solution "The directional derivative ${f}_{u}^{′}(a,b)$ of a function $f(x,y)$ at the point $(a,b)$ in the direction of the vector $v$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{u}^{′}(a,b)=∇f(a,b)\\cdot u\n$$\n\nwhere $u$ is a unit vector in the direction of $v.$\n\nFirst, we find the direction vector and the corresponding unit vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =⟨0,1⟩-⟨1,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,1⟩ \\\\ u & =\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{AB}}{‖\\overset{\\to }{AB}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}}}⟨-1,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨-1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the gradient $∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $A(1,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{e}^{y}+{x}^{2}y),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{e}^{y}+{x}^{2}y)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{e}^{y}+2xy,\\,x{e}^{y}+{x}^{2}⟩ \\\\ ∇\\,f(1,0) & =⟨{e}^{0}+2\\cdot 1\\cdot 0,\\,1\\cdot {e}^{0}+{1}^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the directional derivative is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{u}^{′}(1,0) & =∇f(1,0)\\cdot u \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,2⟩\\cdot ⟨-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}},\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323791 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f931965-4230-53c9-9c4e-51f593f9d015" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321267} {:answer/id #uuid "57f67efd-2b45-5e67-a5e4-4b0fd709ba1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321837} {:answer/id #uuid "7b1a8d46-fbc3-5f92-8b69-6709f7023c5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{80}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322114} {:answer/id #uuid "cda4641b-3880-5a2f-a60a-2d4b84c1090a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322750} {:answer/id #uuid "d4216551-4828-50f1-870a-15d1ba740ddb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{82}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322802}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d4216551-4828-50f1-870a-15d1ba740ddb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{82}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322802} :answer-field/id #uuid "9e6c5b32-a96f-564b-b174-aa4099e65f9a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323476}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "a13cba05-5f4a-5d75-8f65-f72eb54e0d73" :question/math-academy-id "q-36300" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x,y)=2xy+{e}^{3x-y}.$ What is the rate of change of $f(x,y)$ at the point $(1,3)$ in the direction in which the function decreases most rapidly?" :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $f(x,y)$ decreases most rapidly in the opposite direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nIn our case, we compute $-∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(1,3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-∇\\,f(x,y) & =-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2xy+{e}^{3x-y})\\text{ }i-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2xy+{e}^{3x-y})\\text{ }j \\\\  & =-(2y+3{e}^{3x-y})\\text{ }i-(2x-{e}^{3x-y})\\text{ }j \\\\ -∇\\,f(1,3) & =-(2\\cdot 3+3\\cdot {e}^{3\\cdot 1-3})\\text{ }i-(2\\cdot 1-{e}^{3\\cdot 1-3})\\text{ }j \\\\  & =-9\\text{ }i-\\text{ }j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the rate of change of $f(x,y)$ at the point $(1,3)$ in the direction of $-∇f(1,3)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖-∇\\,f(1,3)‖ & =‖-9\\text{ }i-\\text{ }j‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-9)^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{82}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323874 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "255172e6-7a9f-58d5-a331-2f9b1c75fdc1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨3,\\,-\\pi ,\\,3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321437} {:answer/id #uuid "ad71c1c5-8c51-5b0e-a1c2-00e6be326690" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-\\pi +3,\\,\\pi ,\\,3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322506} {:answer/id #uuid "b2583193-7c57-5a7b-a812-d2606cdf10e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨3,\\,0,\\,6⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322542} {:answer/id #uuid "c7d64c08-23f9-5296-9b84-8bb3edc8f8d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-\\pi +3,\\,-1,\\,6⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322698} {:answer/id #uuid "dd715aea-f494-54b7-886a-982c585d2245" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨2,\\,-1,\\,6⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322878}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c7d64c08-23f9-5296-9b84-8bb3edc8f8d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-\\pi +3,\\,-1,\\,6⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322698} :answer-field/id #uuid "511dda4a-e46b-5ff9-8563-b0b60e9db9a5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323325}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "2e292b5a-7460-5589-a8da-d2424c37287d" :question/math-academy-id "q-37080" :question/problem "Given the function $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x+y)+3{z}^{2}x$, in what direction does the function increase most rapidly at the point $(1,0,1)?$" :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $f(x,y,z)$ increases most rapidly in the direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nSo, we compute $∇f(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at the point $(1,0,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x+y)+3{z}^{2}x),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x+y)+3{z}^{2}x),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x+y)+3{z}^{2}x)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x+y)+3{z}^{2},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x+y),\\,6xz⟩ \\\\ ∇f(1,0,1) & =⟨\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi \\cdot 1+0)+3\\cdot {1}^{2},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi \\cdot 1+0),\\,6\\cdot 1\\cdot 1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )+3,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi ),\\,6⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\pi +3,\\,-1,\\,6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function increases most rapidly in the direction of $⟨-\\pi +3,\\,-1,\\,6⟩.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323875 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8837b541-d3d8-5ebe-8ea8-af61591cb6fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322232} {:answer/id #uuid "b6f03f12-e1ac-5472-a11e-f1f87bcd9621" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322572} {:answer/id #uuid "c1717e43-53c5-54f5-8c5f-68df4b9cfd08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322650} {:answer/id #uuid "d2a54f97-4039-5587-a94f-458a55a1776d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322792} {:answer/id #uuid "f7ef68e3-e12b-57d6-890b-83987a1922bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323097}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d2a54f97-4039-5587-a94f-458a55a1776d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322792} :answer-field/id #uuid "4a40605a-36ce-555d-abab-e5a81e672aeb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323308}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "3a0c033d-5079-51f1-a44e-c13c59694293" :question/math-academy-id "q-37083" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x,y)=(x+y)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y).$ What is the rate of change of $f(x,y)$ at the point $(2,-1)$ in the direction in which the function decreases most rapidly?" :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $f(x,y)$ decreases most rapidly in the opposite direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nIn our case, we compute $-∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(2,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-∇\\,f(x,y) & =-\\frac{∂}{∂x}((x+y)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y))\\text{ }i-\\frac{∂}{∂y}((x+y)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y))\\text{ }j \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)+1)\\text{ }i+(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)+1)\\text{ }j \\\\ -∇\\,f(2,-1) & =-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2-1)+1)i-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2-1)+1)j \\\\  & =-i-j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the rate of change of $f(x,y)$ at the point $(2,-1)$ in the direction of $-∇f(2,-1)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖-∇\\,f(2,-1)‖ & =‖-i-j‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351517 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3e2d1818-23b6-5bc6-9705-1aa1f8fcde6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({e}^{-1}+1)\\text{ }i+({e}^{-1}-1)j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349013} {:answer/id #uuid "68eb9247-b932-5869-944e-6c106bd7cb39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(e-1)\\text{ }i+ej" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349413} {:answer/id #uuid "6fd17f28-20e2-5ce1-8906-d302e5e5f909" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({e}^{-1}+1)\\text{ }i+{e}^{-1}j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349472} {:answer/id #uuid "91bd11c8-e407-5439-bdc4-1470bfd9ed1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({e}^{-1}-1)\\text{ }i+{e}^{-1}j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349749} {:answer/id #uuid "df06fa9d-9784-5210-b854-98e07a5502ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(e+1)\\text{ }i+(e-1)j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350399}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "91bd11c8-e407-5439-bdc4-1470bfd9ed1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({e}^{-1}-1)\\text{ }i+{e}^{-1}j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349749} :answer-field/id #uuid "49ce0ac9-9872-520a-b868-69cd398be0c3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350832}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "4e724341-d4b7-5d33-9112-e9d06eaccd62" :question/math-academy-id "q-83554" :question/problem "In what direction does the function $f(x,y)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+y{e}^{x}-xy$ increase most rapidly at the point $(-1,1)?$" :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $f(x,y)$ increases most rapidly in the direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nSo, we compute $∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(-1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+y{e}^{x}-xy)\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+y{e}^{x}-xy)\\text{ }j \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+y{e}^{x}-y)\\text{ }i+(\\frac{x}{y}+{e}^{x}-x)\\text{ }j \\\\ ∇f(1,1) & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡1+1\\cdot {e}^{-1}-1)\\text{ }i+(\\frac{-1}{1}+{e}^{-1}-(-1))\\text{ }j \\\\  & =({e}^{-1}-1)\\text{ }i+{e}^{-1}j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function increases most rapidly in the direction of $({e}^{-1}-1)\\text{ }i+{e}^{-1}j.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351520 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2209d039-cd49-5b32-a5a2-fccba4c4ad04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi \\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348739} {:answer/id #uuid "2f701612-723b-57b5-8841-6b161d28a285" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{\\sqrt{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348867} {:answer/id #uuid "aa56a022-8acc-58dd-9945-dec0f389f84c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349960} {:answer/id #uuid "b2decfd4-db20-5142-a782-96a864cc1cbe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{\\sqrt{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350030} {:answer/id #uuid "eedbad12-eb1d-52b0-b11b-38c97411879b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350537}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2f701612-723b-57b5-8841-6b161d28a285" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{\\sqrt{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348867} :answer-field/id #uuid "b045770b-be7d-51e7-a88e-a4035b50e4fd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351008}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "e60c4cce-b8b4-5a37-8e5f-19c98e4b5e86" :question/math-academy-id "q-83608" :question/problem "Consider the function $g(x,y)=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)}{y}+\\pi xy.$ What is the rate of change of $g(x,y)$ at the point $(1,1)$ in the direction of the vector $v=⟨-2,2⟩?$" :question/worked-solution "The rate of change of $g(x,y)$ at the point $(1,1)$ in the direction of $v$ is equal to the directional derivative ${g}_{u}^{′}(1,1).$ \n\nFirst, we find the unit vector $u$ in the direction of $v:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖ & =\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+{2}^{2}}=2\\sqrt{2} \\\\ u & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨-1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the gradient $∇\\,g(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,g(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)}{y}+\\pi xy),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)}{y}+\\pi xy)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)}{y}+\\pi y,\\,-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)}{{y}^{2}}+\\pi x⟩ \\\\ ∇\\,g(1,1) & =⟨\\frac{\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi \\cdot 1)}{1}+\\pi \\cdot 1,\\,-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi \\cdot 1)}{{1}^{2}}+\\pi \\cdot 1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\pi +\\pi ,0+\\pi ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\pi ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the directional derivative ${g}_{u}^{′}(1,1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}_{u}^{′}(1,1) & =∇\\,g(1,1)\\cdot u \\\\  & =⟨0,\\pi ⟩\\cdot ⟨-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}},\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{0\\cdot (-1)+\\pi \\cdot 1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{\\sqrt{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351521 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "023898fb-a616-56d4-822f-050c9d7ed901" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}(\\pi -2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348471} {:answer/id #uuid "0d1d16bc-4349-5f72-b0c9-a69b046c4e68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(\\pi -2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348560} {:answer/id #uuid "131969f5-9f12-5738-8e7a-92fb804ff6ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi -\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348611} {:answer/id #uuid "92759975-27e9-56dd-8a00-cf955f71c7f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(\\pi +2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349756} {:answer/id #uuid "f2e6ddfc-23e4-505d-a08b-5cfa9e448be9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}(\\pi +2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350577}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "023898fb-a616-56d4-822f-050c9d7ed901" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}(\\pi -2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348471} :answer-field/id #uuid "e4a8d4eb-f7c9-5c5f-88e7-3850845e3558" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351109}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "54b4a6ea-a43d-5fa2-b223-b9c52e67cdd7" :question/math-academy-id "q-83613" :question/problem "Consider the function $g(x,y)=2x\\operatorname{tan}⁡y-2y\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.$ What is the rate of change of $g(x,y)$ at the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ in the direction of the vector $v=⟨-1,1⟩?$" :question/worked-solution "The rate of change of $g(x,y)$ at the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ in the direction of $v$ is equal to the directional derivative $$\n\\displaystyle {g}_{u}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{4}).\n$$ \n\nFirst, we find the unit vector $u$ in the direction of $v:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖ & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}}=\\sqrt{2} \\\\ u & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨-1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the gradient $∇\\,g(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{4}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,g(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x\\operatorname{tan}⁡y-2y\\operatorname{tan}⁡x),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x\\operatorname{tan}⁡y-2y\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2\\operatorname{tan}⁡y-2y{sec}^{2}⁡x,\\,2x{sec}^{2}⁡y-2\\operatorname{tan}⁡x⟩ \\\\ ∇\\,g(\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =⟨2\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)-2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}\\cdot {sec}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right),\\,2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}\\cdot {sec}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)-2\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2\\cdot 1-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot 2,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot 2-2\\cdot 1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2-\\pi ,\\,\\pi -2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the directional derivative $$\n\\displaystyle {g}_{u}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{4})\n$$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}_{u}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =∇\\,g(\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\cdot u \\\\  & =⟨2-\\pi ,\\pi -2⟩\\cdot ⟨-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}},\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{(2-\\pi )\\cdot (-1)+(\\pi -2)\\cdot 1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pi -4}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}(\\pi -2).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354470 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1304514c-b435-522f-ad3a-591f2559a22b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11\\sqrt{34}}{34}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351730} {:answer/id #uuid "7e4fba7a-fe7c-5a9c-9fbe-87782d23df70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11\\sqrt{43}}{43}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352676} {:answer/id #uuid "c8a1b502-7fbf-5960-aaec-5f60565ea22e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{10\\sqrt{34}}{34}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353314} {:answer/id #uuid "c94460ac-494e-513e-82ed-c599cb03d029" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{10\\sqrt{43}}{34}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353320} {:answer/id #uuid "f1e9e528-e824-57bb-899a-9e75210a8db6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{34}}{34}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353676}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c8a1b502-7fbf-5960-aaec-5f60565ea22e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{10\\sqrt{34}}{34}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353314} :answer-field/id #uuid "e9f8833b-3f19-5c49-a196-1b1b54521800" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354216}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f8f45df0-069c-5e4a-a4d7-bdd2e15f5467" :question/math-academy-id "q-86429" :question/problem "Find the directional derivative of the function $f(x,y)=6{x}^{2}+2y$ at the point $(0,1)$ in the direction of the vector $v=3i+5j.$" :question/worked-solution "The directional derivative ${f}_{u}^{′}(a,b)$ of a function $f(x,y)$ at the point $(a,b)$ in the direction of the vector $v$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{u}^{′}(a,b)=∇f(a,b)\\cdot u\n$$\n\nwhere $u$ is a unit vector in the direction of $v.$\n\nFirst, we find the unit vector $u$ in the direction of $v:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖ & =\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{5}^{2}}=\\sqrt{34} \\\\ u & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{34}}(3i+5j)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the gradient $∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(0,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(6{x}^{2}+2y)\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(6{x}^{2}+2y)\\text{ }j \\\\  & =12x\\,i+2\\,j \\\\ ∇\\,f(0,1) & =(12\\cdot 0)\\,i+2\\,j \\\\  & =2j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the directional derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{u}^{′}(0,1) & =∇\\,f(0,1)\\cdot u \\\\  & =2j\\cdot (\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{34}}i+\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{34}}j) \\\\  & =\\frac{0\\cdot 3+2\\cdot 5}{\\sqrt{34}} \\\\  & =\\frac{10\\sqrt{34}}{34}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354478 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1721b07e-259c-5a3f-a8e9-4f2d59c63517" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351772} {:answer/id #uuid "215ecffb-b146-5627-91b0-550f671dd2c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351867} {:answer/id #uuid "722664ed-33e9-5cc1-b19b-4351c6efb19f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i+j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352566} {:answer/id #uuid "81a3d5cd-c8fc-5c45-bcca-96eb05f8c947" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2i-j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352695} {:answer/id #uuid "df442e6f-48af-5860-ac3c-76c823ada575" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i-j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353507}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1721b07e-259c-5a3f-a8e9-4f2d59c63517" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351772} :answer-field/id #uuid "40e73d7d-cb49-50d9-9ae1-9e40c00f9a4e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353927}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d1b68e57-d9ea-56e4-b3b2-4bb6dcbf9139" :question/math-academy-id "q-86537" :question/problem "In what direction does the function $f(x,y)=3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ increase most rapidly at the point $(0,1)?$" :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $f(x,y)$ increases most rapidly in the direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nSo, we compute $∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(0,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\text{ }j \\\\  & =(y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+6x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)\\text{ }i+(3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\text{ }j \\\\ ∇f(0,1) & =(1\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+6\\cdot 0\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(1))\\text{ }i+(3(0)^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0))\\text{ }j \\\\  & =-j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function increases most rapidly in the direction of $-j.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446239 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e7ee258-aeb5-5bdf-92b6-350be2acf019" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{12}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446182} {:answer/id #uuid "2f930ebf-9d00-57ce-9436-e66479caf16c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446183} {:answer/id #uuid "5b48cb68-dd75-5ec3-b835-523ae565ed53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446196} {:answer/id #uuid "d6b0abaf-e4dd-5cab-82a0-5a8354af1953" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{8}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446226} {:answer/id #uuid "de3f161a-4d79-5e42-a5c8-9b89385ad3f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{16}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446227}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "de3f161a-4d79-5e42-a5c8-9b89385ad3f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{16}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446227} :answer-field/id #uuid "33218d7b-e61f-5da1-bb1d-d60915785e82" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446230}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "30b363a1-05a8-5997-8feb-6bd8aaa30fa0" :question/math-academy-id "q-83609" :question/problem "Consider the function $g(x,y)=xy{e}^{x+y}+{x}^{2}y.$ What is the rate of change of $g(x,y)$ at the point $(1,-1)$ in the direction of the vector $v=⟨3,-4⟩?$" :question/worked-solution "The rate of change of $g(x,y)$ at the point $(1,-1)$ in the direction of $v$ is equal to the directional derivative ${g}_{u}^{′}(1,-1).$ \n\nFirst, we find the unit vector $u$ in the direction of $v:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖ & =\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+(-4)^{2}}=\\sqrt{25}=5 \\\\ u & =\\frac{1}{5}⟨3,-4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the gradient $∇\\,g(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(1,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,g(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy{e}^{x+y}+{x}^{2}y),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy{e}^{x+y}+{x}^{2}y)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨y{e}^{x+y}+xy{e}^{x+y}+2xy,\\,x{e}^{x+y}+xy{e}^{x+y}+{x}^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨y(1+x){e}^{x+y}+2xy,\\,x(1+y){e}^{x+y}+{x}^{2}⟩ \\\\ ∇\\,g(1,-1) & =⟨(-1)\\cdot (1+1){e}^{1-1}+2\\cdot 1\\cdot (-1),\\,1\\cdot (1-1){e}^{1-1}+{1}^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-4,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the directional derivative ${g}_{u}^{′}(1,-1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}_{u}^{′}(1,-1) & =∇\\,g(1,1)\\cdot u \\\\  & =⟨-4,1⟩\\cdot ⟨\\frac{3}{5},-\\frac{4}{5}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{(-4)\\cdot 3+1\\cdot (-4)}{5} \\\\  & =-\\frac{16}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446240 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4a640dc4-f0d6-5221-9834-4d0e7ba9f870" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-3,\\,-4,\\,4⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446191} {:answer/id #uuid "8b8bfd4d-1ee5-507f-b0d0-bf0d05bf14af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,\\,-2,\\,2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446210} {:answer/id #uuid "8e8a41f1-5149-587a-ba0e-8dfbff363aab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨3,\\,4,\\,-4⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446212} {:answer/id #uuid "a78d148d-8a70-55a2-a873-68980ae2bd0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-1,\\,2,\\,-2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446215} {:answer/id #uuid "b0d6b2ea-bb17-5bc1-89b4-f847433baa23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,\\,-2,\\,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446217}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8b8bfd4d-1ee5-507f-b0d0-bf0d05bf14af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,\\,-2,\\,2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446210} :answer-field/id #uuid "da37f824-4e73-5b4a-a18f-250ab74de323" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446237}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "3642d7fd-0113-5cbf-a61e-17061219f8d0" :question/math-academy-id "q-83598" :question/problem "In what direction does the function $g(x,y,z)=x{y}^{2}-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+z)$ decrease most rapidly at the point $(1,1,1)?$" :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $g(x,y,z)$ decreases most rapidly in the direction opposite to that of its gradient.\n\nSo, we compute $-∇g(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at the point $(1,1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-∇g(x,y,z) & =-⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{y}^{2}+4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+z)),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{y}^{2}+4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+z)),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x{y}^{2}+4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+z))⟩ \\\\  & =-⟨{y}^{2}-\\frac{4}{x+z},\\,2xy,\\,-\\frac{4}{x+z}⟩ \\\\ -∇g(1,1,1) & =-⟨{1}^{2}-\\frac{4}{1+1},\\,2\\cdot 1\\cdot 1,\\,-\\frac{4}{1+1}⟩ \\\\  & =-⟨-1,\\,2,\\,-2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,-2,\\,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function decreases most rapidly in the direction of $⟨1,\\,-2,\\,2⟩.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446241 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4d2f5038-f42a-5fd7-a95d-a297da774c15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446193} {:answer/id #uuid "a9d07651-85d5-5624-ae13-7a41e816c585" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{8}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446216} {:answer/id #uuid "b248e7e5-413b-5466-b7ed-293308db0e18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446218} {:answer/id #uuid "d058285d-919c-56d4-bc37-2e175c0f050a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446225} {:answer/id #uuid "eb4ac023-e663-5e98-b2d3-aa3c2ec67064" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446228}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d058285d-919c-56d4-bc37-2e175c0f050a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446225} :answer-field/id #uuid "cea3cee4-a045-5dc7-8596-6cf26a0cb161" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446236}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "4c3374df-275b-5fb9-8864-40efeec8b5b4" :question/math-academy-id "q-36306" :question/problem "Given the function  $g(x,y)=({x}^{2}+y)^{2}$, find its directional derivative at the\npoint $A(-1,1)$ in the direction that's towards the point $B(3,4).$" :question/worked-solution "The directional derivative ${f}_{u}^{′}(a,b)$ of a function $f(x,y)$ at the point $(a,b)$ in the direction of the vector $v$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{u}^{′}(a,b)=∇f(a,b)\\cdot u\n$$\n\nwhere $u$ is a unit vector in the direction of $v.$\n\nFirst, we find the direction vector and the corresponding unit vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =⟨3,4⟩-⟨-1,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4,3⟩ \\\\ u & =\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{AB}}{‖\\overset{\\to }{AB}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{4}^{2}}}⟨4,3⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}⟨4,3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the gradient $∇g(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $A(-1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇g(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+y)^{2},\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+y)^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4x({x}^{2}+y),\\,2({x}^{2}+y)⟩ \\\\ ∇g(-1,1) & =⟨4\\cdot (-1)\\cdot ((-1)^{2}+1),2\\cdot ((-1)^{2}+1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-8,4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the directional derivative is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}_{u}^{′}(-1,1) & =∇g(-1,1)\\cdot u \\\\  & =⟨-8,4⟩\\cdot ⟨\\frac{4}{5},\\frac{3}{5}⟩ \\\\  & =-8\\cdot \\frac{4}{5}+4\\cdot \\frac{3}{5} \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446242 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "021bf1f8-b881-536a-a8f1-f59bbd10c0cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{18}{5\\sqrt{13}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446176} {:answer/id #uuid "455dead3-5a38-5f75-bb6f-3603611372d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446188} {:answer/id #uuid "502f0fe4-aa68-585a-bbd4-188d4c9372d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7\\sqrt{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446195} {:answer/id #uuid "5d9b9f76-b123-5719-bc4e-29ca3f63e30e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446197} {:answer/id #uuid "b8aca910-6177-50b0-976d-f5a0cad4d3f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446220}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "021bf1f8-b881-536a-a8f1-f59bbd10c0cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{18}{5\\sqrt{13}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446176} :answer-field/id #uuid "be838696-b1cd-5c8f-81ac-b0d86172dd65" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446235}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5fc2a3e0-1de4-50ca-b644-c592b3edb577" :question/math-academy-id "q-130268" :question/problem "Find the directional derivative of the function $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}$ at the point $A(4,3)$ in the direction towards the point $B(1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "The directional derivative ${f}_{u}^{′}(a,b)$ of a function $f(x,y)$ at the point $(a,b)$ in the direction of the vector $v$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{u}^{′}(a,b)=∇f(a,b)\\cdot u\n$$\n\nwhere $u$ is a unit vector in the direction of $v.$\n\nFirst, we find the direction vector and the corresponding unit vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =⟨1,1⟩-⟨4,3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-3,-2⟩ \\\\ u & =\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{AB}}{‖\\overset{\\to }{AB}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+(-2)^{2}}}⟨-3,-2⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}⟨-3,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the gradient $∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $A(4,3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}},\\,\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}⟩ \\\\ ∇f(4,3) & =⟨\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+{3}^{2}}},\\,\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+{3}^{2}}}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{4}{5},\\frac{3}{5}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the directional derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{u}^{′}(4,3) & =∇f(4,3)\\cdot u \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{4}{5},\\frac{3}{5}⟩\\cdot ⟨-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}},-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{4(-3)+3(-2)}{5\\sqrt{13}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{18}{5\\sqrt{13}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446243 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4eb318ef-e5da-50bb-852d-abf0124a9413" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446194} {:answer/id #uuid "8d1128a0-3bc8-5ee6-8690-02b7875e3982" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446211} {:answer/id #uuid "94e3a4fd-f234-5419-b576-fa4fcdfcf2eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446213} {:answer/id #uuid "c41e1274-b58c-591c-ba2b-30404efa267c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446221} {:answer/id #uuid "c4350363-1f04-5bba-b31a-249136e6fc8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446222}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8d1128a0-3bc8-5ee6-8690-02b7875e3982" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446211} :answer-field/id #uuid "716b4932-e73e-59f5-9ec5-4b4ed730dece" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446232}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "72e3df1a-6338-564c-adc2-85283b26953d" :question/math-academy-id "q-130811" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x,y)=2\\sqrt{xy}.$ What is the rate of change of $f(x,y)$ at the point $(8,2)$ in the direction in which the function decreases most rapidly?" :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $f(x,y)$ decreases most rapidly in the opposite direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nIn our case, we compute $-∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(8,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-∇f(x,y) & =-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2\\sqrt{xy})i-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2\\sqrt{xy})j \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{\\frac{y}{x}}\\,i-\\sqrt{\\frac{x}{y}}\\,j \\\\ -∇f(8,2) & =-\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{8}}\\,i-\\sqrt{\\frac{8}{2}}\\,j \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\,i-2\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the rate of change of $f(x,y)$ at the point $(8,2)$ in the direction of $-∇f(8,2)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖-∇f(8,2)‖ & =‖-\\frac{1}{2}\\,i-2\\,j‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{2}+(-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4}+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{17}{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446244 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b6f9c9c-fd05-533b-ab92-794682300821" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446177} {:answer/id #uuid "19005152-b76d-5f3e-8e83-f9fa12abf908" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446181} {:answer/id #uuid "3b5f201e-9679-590f-9849-011ab1fefcb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446185} {:answer/id #uuid "60b225d0-997a-5fbc-a18b-ec3094252266" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446201} {:answer/id #uuid "86d2d586-ece9-5dd8-9965-84273626e130" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{5}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446209}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0b6f9c9c-fd05-533b-ab92-794682300821" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446177} :answer-field/id #uuid "4a49be9b-1ed8-506c-ad9a-611025859067" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446231}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "754f0abc-16b3-51ff-9446-aa81fd4c05d3" :question/math-academy-id "q-130804" :question/problem "Consider the function $g(x,y)=4{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2}.$ What is the rate of change of $g(x,y)$ at the point $(2,1)$ in the direction of the vector $v=⟨5,12⟩?$" :question/worked-solution "The rate of change of $g(x,y)$ at the point $(2,1)$ in the direction of $v$ is equal to the directional derivative ${g}_{u}^{′}(2,1).$ \n\nFirst, we find the unit vector $u$ in the direction of $v:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖ & =\\sqrt{{5}^{2}+{12}^{2}}=13 \\\\ u & =\\frac{1}{13}⟨5,12⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the gradient $∇g(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(2,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇g(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨8x,\\,-6y⟩ \\\\ ∇g(2,1) & =⟨8(2),-6(1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨16,-6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the directional derivative ${g}_{u}^{′}(2,1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}_{u}^{′}(2,1) & =∇g(2,1)\\cdot u \\\\  & =⟨16,-6⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{13}⟨5,12⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{16(5)-6(12)}{13} \\\\  & =\\frac{80-72}{13} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446245 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "40cd1b3b-3226-567f-8df7-b4203af0e8e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446186} {:answer/id #uuid "78161357-66de-58ad-955b-760220e2284c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446204} {:answer/id #uuid "7ced684c-4ebe-5a34-b02d-a8bda555a9f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446205} {:answer/id #uuid "7dfd9ca6-8542-5ff2-910b-fbe85fdf3f9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446207} {:answer/id #uuid "a3dd3ec0-5bd2-592f-a2b9-507da0e25254" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446214}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7ced684c-4ebe-5a34-b02d-a8bda555a9f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446205} :answer-field/id #uuid "e6dc0783-a199-5ca8-ace2-ec2c865c730a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446238}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "7d39de67-7fd2-5aeb-a063-e9cf738816af" :question/math-academy-id "q-130806" :question/problem "Consider the function $g(x,y)={x}^{y}.$ What is the rate of change of $g(x,y)$ at the point $(1,1)$ in the direction in which the function increases most rapidly?" :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $g(x,y)$ increases most rapidly in the direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nIn our case, we compute $∇g(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,g(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{y}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{y})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨y{x}^{y-1},\\,{x}^{y}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x⟩ \\\\ ∇\\,g(1,1) & =⟨(1){1}^{1-1},\\,({1}^{1})\\operatorname{ln}⁡1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the rate of change of $g(x,y)$ at the point $(1,1)$ in the direction of $∇g(1,1)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖∇\\,g(1,1)‖ & =‖⟨1,0⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446246 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e31501d-4531-5b76-9877-d2a3acbb5d11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-6,1,-3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446178} {:answer/id #uuid "314f45e4-cf29-50b1-9428-b22c82951ca6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-2,-5,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446184} {:answer/id #uuid "4318aea0-1f2c-58b7-bdd1-6b19fbc76a15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,2,-4⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446187} {:answer/id #uuid "46bb65c1-8132-59c6-9f4b-6909dff7bc8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,-1,4⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446189} {:answer/id #uuid "f1d20d6e-c59b-5e2f-b608-e625a0868759" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-3,2,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446229}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0e31501d-4531-5b76-9877-d2a3acbb5d11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-6,1,-3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446178} :answer-field/id #uuid "8901f1a6-9dd3-5894-ab03-0ab176fa8fea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446234}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "beaf6ecd-5ac6-5efb-ad1b-41d64d5bbd40" :question/math-academy-id "q-130273" :question/problem "In what direction does the function $g(x,y,z)=10-3{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-{z}^{3}$ increase most rapidly at the point $(1,-\\frac{1}{2},-1)?$" :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $g(x,y,z)$ increases most rapidly in the direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nSo, we compute $∇g(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at the point $(1,-\\frac{1}{2},-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇g(x,y,z) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(10-3{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-{z}^{3}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}(10-3{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-{z}^{3}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂z}(10-3{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-{z}^{3})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-6x,\\,-2y,\\,-3{z}^{2}⟩ \\\\ ∇g(1,-\\frac{1}{2},-1) & =⟨-6(1),-2\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right),-3(-1)^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-6,1,-3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function increases most rapidly in the direction of $⟨-6,1,-3⟩.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446247 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01fea9f5-7ff4-5f0d-b619-5f87a6a90c35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨2{e}^{4},\\,2{e}^{2}+3,\\,12⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446175} {:answer/id #uuid "4cc668bd-9c56-5b86-a394-e8a7b8069688" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨2e,\\,{e}^{4}-3,\\,12⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446192} {:answer/id #uuid "604a5840-27db-5147-b4e6-7928a771885b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨4{e}^{2},\\,2{e}^{4}-3,\\,-12⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446200} {:answer/id #uuid "682d87e6-5a4b-538f-8f34-c5cd1f0d46e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨2{e}^{4},\\,2{e}^{4}+3,\\,-12⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446202} {:answer/id #uuid "c9528b5a-8771-5176-a89b-c59ec1b7b7ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨2{e}^{4},\\,2e-3,\\,-21⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446223}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "682d87e6-5a4b-538f-8f34-c5cd1f0d46e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨2{e}^{4},\\,2{e}^{4}+3,\\,-12⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446202} :answer-field/id #uuid "845bc3ce-d9e8-5c27-8b7e-c01d5d7a2ced" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446233}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f9974a87-21df-58c9-86ba-41826947b876" :question/math-academy-id "q-86539" :question/problem "In what direction does the function $g(x,y,z)={e}^{xy}+3{z}^{2}y$ increase most rapidly at the point $(2,2,-1)?$" :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $g(x,y,z)$ increases most rapidly in the direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nSo, we compute $∇g(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at the point $(2,2,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇g(x,y,z) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{xy}+3{z}^{2}y),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{xy}+3{z}^{2}y),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂z}({e}^{xy}+3{z}^{2}y)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨y{e}^{xy},\\,x{e}^{xy}+3{z}^{2},\\,6yz⟩ \\\\ ∇g(2,2,-1) & =⟨2\\cdot {e}^{2\\cdot 2},\\,2\\cdot {e}^{2\\cdot 2}+3(-1)^{2},\\,6\\cdot 2\\cdot (-1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2{e}^{4},\\,2{e}^{4}+3,\\,-12⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function increases most rapidly in the direction of $⟨2{e}^{4},\\,2{e}^{4}+3,\\,-12⟩.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501150 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "172f3f98-43e9-586f-8bdf-a9ddf4ae061e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\,i-12\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500519} {:answer/id #uuid "6e5dcfde-da03-5e9b-8257-b3a15610fd0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\,i+18\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500690} {:answer/id #uuid "9305e79c-d3f6-5049-b273-0bc89befd2f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3\\,i+4\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500772} {:answer/id #uuid "9b2acabe-a8aa-5bd5-a28c-992a7b2ef314" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5\\,i+3\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500782} {:answer/id #uuid "e95111ed-e4a8-5a81-b040-27eaaa405bc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i+9\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500966}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6e5dcfde-da03-5e9b-8257-b3a15610fd0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\,i+18\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500690} :answer-field/id #uuid "35227601-10b3-594c-aac4-a002a6f61ca3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501037}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "1c3c8fd2-c84f-5ffa-82e1-4f53ac4509f6" :question/math-academy-id "q-130277" :question/problem "In what direction does the function $f(x,y)=2(xy+1)^{3/2}$ increase most rapidly at the point $(3,1)?$" :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $f(x,y)$ increases most rapidly in the direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nSo, we compute $∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(3,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2(xy+1)^{3/2})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2(xy+1)^{3/2})\\,j \\\\  & =3y\\sqrt{xy+1}\\,i+3x\\sqrt{xy+1}\\,j \\\\ ∇f(3,1) & =3(1)\\sqrt{(3)(1)+1}\\,i+3(3)\\sqrt{(3)(1)+1}\\,j \\\\  & =6\\,i+18\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function increases most rapidly in the direction of $6\\,i+18\\,j.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501201 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13f0ad0a-171c-5322-8cf2-cfca08972fdb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{e\\sqrt{5}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500510} {:answer/id #uuid "407711da-42ac-56dc-a3f6-af974631b602" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500594} {:answer/id #uuid "5490207d-8efb-5f97-80cd-1bd0f27685fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{e\\sqrt{5}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500639} {:answer/id #uuid "86e21ca8-366b-5985-a073-18a5cd00683d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500749} {:answer/id #uuid "8d4c7ab4-1278-5730-804a-3c97ca86d365" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{e\\sqrt{5}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500762}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5490207d-8efb-5f97-80cd-1bd0f27685fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{e\\sqrt{5}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500639} :answer-field/id #uuid "fcae1503-ee5b-5bcc-859b-64edce5a1f65" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501132}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a3fef551-e9bd-56f6-a5fb-6131de39b9cb" :question/math-academy-id "q-86542" :question/problem "Consider the function $g(x,y)=\\frac{{e}^{y}}{yx}.$ What is the rate of change of $g(x,y)$ at the point $(1,1)$ in the direction of the vector $v=⟨2,4⟩?$" :question/worked-solution "The rate of change of $g(x,y)$ at the point $(1,1)$ in the direction of $v$ is equal to the directional derivative ${g}_{u}^{′}(1,1).$ \n\nFirst, we find the unit vector $u$ in the direction of $v:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖ & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{4}^{2}}=\\sqrt{20}=2\\sqrt{5} \\\\ u & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{5}}⟨2,4⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}⟨1,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the gradient $∇g(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇g(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}\\left(\\frac{{e}^{y}}{yx}\\right),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}\\left(\\frac{{e}^{y}}{yx}\\right)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{{e}^{y}}{{x}^{2}y},\\,\\frac{{e}^{y}(y-1)}{x{y}^{2}}⟩ \\\\ ∇g(1,1) & =⟨-\\frac{{e}^{1}}{{1}^{2}\\cdot 1},\\,\\frac{{e}^{1}\\cdot (1-1)}{1\\cdot {1}^{2}}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-e,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the directional derivative ${g}_{u}^{′}(1,1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}_{u}^{′}(1,1) & =∇g(1,1)\\cdot u \\\\  & =⟨-e,0⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}⟨1,2⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}(-e\\cdot 1+0\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =-\\frac{e}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{e\\sqrt{5}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501227 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08fb6012-956b-5c39-8935-74645f89ee82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\pi }^{2}\\,i+2\\pi \\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500483} {:answer/id #uuid "9f0516fc-fa33-5a32-bc85-021583caaccd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\pi }^{2}\\,i-j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500790} {:answer/id #uuid "a91d4692-51f1-5ef6-96a3-68a85bcad337" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i-\\pi \\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500817} {:answer/id #uuid "b20c5f55-306b-5cef-9a67-804b11be245c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i-{\\pi }^{2}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500840} {:answer/id #uuid "f8c25011-b246-57b2-afb4-9949bd083e31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi \\,i+\\pi \\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500997}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b20c5f55-306b-5cef-9a67-804b11be245c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i-{\\pi }^{2}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500840} :answer-field/id #uuid "941d1058-20a9-5c97-a5b7-7c5da913add0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501084}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d2926681-2f02-501e-9c4c-db877ffeff07" :question/math-academy-id "q-83562" :question/problem "In what direction does the function $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+{x}^{2}y$ decrease most rapidly at the point $(\\pi ,0)?$" :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $f(x,y)$ decreases most rapidly in the direction opposite to that of its gradient.\n\nSo, we compute $-∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(\\pi ,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-∇f(x,y) & =-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+{x}^{2}y)\\text{ }i-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+{x}^{2}y)\\text{ }j \\\\  & =-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+2xy)\\text{ }i-(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+{x}^{2})\\text{ }j \\\\ -∇f(\\pi ,0) & =-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡0+2\\cdot \\pi \\cdot 0)\\text{ }i+(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡0+{\\pi }^{2})\\text{ }j \\\\  & =i-{\\pi }^{2}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function decreases most rapidly in the direction of $i-{\\pi }^{2}\\,j.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504655 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a4055cd-dc46-58cf-9414-ebd3aeced26d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,i-\\sqrt{\\pi }\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504052} {:answer/id #uuid "169a9454-865f-5c99-afad-24018fd51b22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi \\,i-j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504072} {:answer/id #uuid "24013cd6-fd21-5ea4-a444-92864f2d2b61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\pi \\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504094} {:answer/id #uuid "24c7ddcb-f4c6-55ee-80f6-45bc59cf1057" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{\\pi }\\,i+2\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504096} {:answer/id #uuid "2fefcbb7-1707-5b33-9ea0-368b42c461ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\,i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504115}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "24c7ddcb-f4c6-55ee-80f6-45bc59cf1057" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{\\pi }\\,i+2\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504096} :answer-field/id #uuid "ba947109-a6e8-5648-9e45-f1bf117b385d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504568}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "93366598-9424-5753-a6aa-f85435a0ef2f" :question/math-academy-id "q-130279" :question/problem "In what direction does the function $f(x,y)={e}^{2y}\\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}^{2})$ increase most rapidly at the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{\\pi }}{2},0)?$" :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $f(x,y)$ increases most rapidly in the direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nSo, we compute $∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(1,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{2y}\\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}^{2}))\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{2y}\\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}^{2}))\\,j \\\\  & =2x{e}^{2y}{sec}^{2}⁡({x}^{2})\\,i+2{e}^{2y}\\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}^{2})\\,j \\\\ ∇f(\\frac{\\sqrt{\\pi }}{2},0) & =2\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{\\pi }}{2}\\right){e}^{0}{sec}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)\\,i+2{e}^{0}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)\\,j \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{\\pi }\\,i+2\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function increases most rapidly in the direction of $2\\sqrt{\\pi }\\,i+2\\,j.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518124 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05e252ff-bff2-5a0c-8722-678c22a7c16c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6\\sqrt{5}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517501} {:answer/id #uuid "070f81d7-bbdd-59f5-971a-c1e85fd656fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\sqrt{10}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517503} {:answer/id #uuid "3a8dff85-9179-52c1-a1eb-e2b80b8d392e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5\\sqrt{10}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517603} {:answer/id #uuid "636cdbc8-2c6a-553b-a040-1b1eb24c4aed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517671} {:answer/id #uuid "9a89bbd5-05e0-5b9e-875f-8fbff4dcb441" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6\\sqrt{10}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517783}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9a89bbd5-05e0-5b9e-875f-8fbff4dcb441" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6\\sqrt{10}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517783} :answer-field/id #uuid "fa6bc3a1-b754-5de6-a15a-17b5690dd1bc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518094}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "36bc1d86-9d95-5fad-8592-9df3687ca62a" :question/math-academy-id "q-86533" :question/problem "Find the directional derivative of the function $f(x,y)=(2{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2})^{2}$ at the point $A(-1,-1)$ in the direction towards the point $B(2,0).$" :question/worked-solution "The directional derivative ${f}_{u}^{′}(a,b)$ of a function $f(x,y)$ at the point $(a,b)$ in the direction of the vector $v$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{u}^{′}(a,b)=∇f(a,b)\\cdot u\n$$\n\nwhere $u$ is a unit vector in the direction of $v.$\n\nFirst, we find the direction vector and the corresponding unit vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =⟨2,0⟩-⟨-1,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,1⟩ \\\\ u & =\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{AB}}{‖\\overset{\\to }{AB}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{1}^{2}}}⟨3,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}⟨3,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the gradient $∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $A(-1,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2})^{2},\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2})^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨8x(2{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2}),\\,36{y}^{3}-24{x}^{2}y⟩ \\\\ ∇f(-1,-1) & =⟨8(-1)\\cdot (2(-1)^{2}-3(-1)^{2}),36(-1)^{3}-24(-1)^{2}(-1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨8,-12⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the directional derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{u}^{′}(-1,-1) & =∇f(-1,-1)\\cdot u \\\\  & =⟨8,-12⟩\\cdot ⟨\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}},\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}⟩ \\\\  & =8\\cdot \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}-12\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6\\sqrt{10}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518155 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5e8ca8f9-1d26-55d7-9b86-8c52d09b0ce5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-⟨\\frac{2}{3},\\,\\frac{2}{3},\\,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517660} {:answer/id #uuid "a9ea4930-c661-5fcb-a591-ae748e162fef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{3}{2},\\,\\frac{3}{2},\\,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517804} {:answer/id #uuid "ac6eb5e3-7ae1-506a-bc7d-d480b4fe04c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{2}{3},\\,-\\frac{2}{3},\\,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517807} {:answer/id #uuid "c9a6152f-0b20-5bb6-96d5-09c1f86baab1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{2}{3},\\,-\\frac{2}{3},\\,2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517870} {:answer/id #uuid "eac115c2-4ed8-51a6-aa73-4f0569e0d56e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{3}{2},\\,-\\frac{2}{3},\\,2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517934}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ac6eb5e3-7ae1-506a-bc7d-d480b4fe04c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{2}{3},\\,-\\frac{2}{3},\\,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517807} :answer-field/id #uuid "082380e4-f20f-513e-9d31-b936b5054d9b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517972}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "6d63b608-6564-54eb-94c2-8afc07416275" :question/math-academy-id "q-86540" :question/problem "In what direction does the function $g(x,y,z)=4{z}^{2}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-y)$ increase most rapidly at the point $(2,-1,0)?$" :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $g(x,y,z)$ increases most rapidly in the direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nSo, we compute $∇g(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at the point $(2,-1,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇g(x,y,z) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4{z}^{2}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-y)),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4{z}^{2}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-y)),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂z}(4{z}^{2}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-y))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{2}{x-y},\\,-\\frac{2}{x-y},\\,8z⟩ \\\\ ∇g(2,-1,0) & =⟨\\frac{2}{2-(-1)},\\,-\\frac{2}{2-(-1)},\\,8\\cdot 0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{2}{3},\\,-\\frac{2}{3},\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function increases most rapidly in the direction of $⟨\\frac{2}{3},\\,-\\frac{2}{3},\\,0⟩.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518218 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "215d83fe-4933-5de8-986e-15f0aee87618" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨4,-3,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517546} {:answer/id #uuid "2d55e643-74fe-5152-bfd8-c76baf2debc2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-3,-1,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517574} {:answer/id #uuid "6b204e6b-3938-557e-9c36-e6465fac0526" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,2,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517687} {:answer/id #uuid "9c5a1144-2bb6-52a6-ae6a-f5e1b580463b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨2,0,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517790} {:answer/id #uuid "f5758c55-ad74-5dfd-b215-f4a7c6749383" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,3,-2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517955}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "215d83fe-4933-5de8-986e-15f0aee87618" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨4,-3,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517546} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a0c283d-e80d-5a92-84dc-65204c4fbaa7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518044}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f39e417c-6152-5bd7-a05f-5fd83a9d6cdc" :question/math-academy-id "q-130272" :question/problem "In what direction does the function $g(x,y,z)=x{z}^{2}-{y}^{3}$ increase most rapidly at the point $(0,1,2)?$" :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $g(x,y,z)$ increases most rapidly in the direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nSo, we compute $∇g(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at the point $(0,1,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇g(x,y,z) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{z}^{2}-{y}^{3}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{z}^{2}-{y}^{3}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x{z}^{2}-{y}^{3})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{z}^{2},\\,-3{y}^{2},\\,2xz⟩ \\\\ ∇g(0,1,2) & =⟨{2}^{2},-3(1)^{2},2(0)(2)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4,-3,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function increases most rapidly in the direction of $⟨4,-3,0⟩.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523202 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10175385-4f52-58f4-a77c-fcc932f5b130" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4 \\sin{(1 )}\\,i+2 \\cos{(1 )}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523193} {:answer/id #uuid "32b0c782-0ae8-567b-8b5c-f140f678674a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8 \\sin{(1 )}\\,i+2 \\cos{(1 )}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523200} {:answer/id #uuid "4a6a4fac-5734-5eb0-9ed6-a928757ce0a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8 \\sin{(1 )}\\,i-6 \\cos{(1 )}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523203} {:answer/id #uuid "78649c28-3f6b-5c90-bcdf-d6aae7f64441" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2 \\cos{(1 )}\\,i-8 \\sin{(1 )}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523213} {:answer/id #uuid "a4c875e9-9286-5098-a619-c210f8e79f4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8 \\sin{(1 )}\\,i-2 \\cos{(1 )}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523220}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "32b0c782-0ae8-567b-8b5c-f140f678674a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8 \\sin{(1 )}\\,i+2 \\cos{(1 )}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523200} :answer-field/id #uuid "502f7d26-3a3c-5b78-942c-f5b26d010ea8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523207}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "48a5b0a5-ecb9-5e4e-bbb7-138d96fc81cd" :question/problem "In what direction does the function $f(x,y)=2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-4y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ decrease most rapidly at the point $(1,1)?$" :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $f(x,y)$ decreases most rapidly in the direction opposite to that of its gradient.\n\nSo, we compute $∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-4y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-4y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\text{ }j \\\\  & =(4y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+4x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)\\text{ }i+(2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\text{ }j \\\\ ∇f(1,1) & =(4\\cdot (1\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(1))+4\\cdot 1\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(1))\\text{ }i+(2(1)^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1)-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1))\\text{ }j \\\\  & =8 \\sin{(1 )}\\text{ }i-2 \\cos{(1 )}\\text{ }j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function decreases most rapidly in the direction of $-8 \\sin{(1 )}\\,i+2 \\cos{(1 )}\\,j.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523211 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2880b2e8-4f16-5d70-bb12-a638f2d32836" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨3,\\,2,\\,4⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523199} {:answer/id #uuid "4e8a0998-482a-549c-b11c-b4cdf16a0a4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{3}{29},\\,\\frac{2}{29},\\,\\frac{4}{29}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523206} {:answer/id #uuid "65dd24b1-670c-595e-ac35-85b55310a28c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{\\sqrt{21}}{21},\\,\\frac{2 \\sqrt{21}}{21},\\,\\frac{4 \\sqrt{21}}{21}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523210} {:answer/id #uuid "9dc035af-ed1b-551a-8b01-10f5d3b91125" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-\\frac{3 \\sqrt{29}}{29},\\,-\\frac{2 \\sqrt{29}}{29},\\,-\\frac{4 \\sqrt{29}}{29}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523219} {:answer/id #uuid "eaec32c4-2c02-5eff-a1fa-2a8399d25d1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{3 \\sqrt{29}}{29},\\,\\frac{2 \\sqrt{29}}{29},\\,\\frac{4 \\sqrt{29}}{29}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523232}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eaec32c4-2c02-5eff-a1fa-2a8399d25d1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{3 \\sqrt{29}}{29},\\,\\frac{2 \\sqrt{29}}{29},\\,\\frac{4 \\sqrt{29}}{29}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523232} :answer-field/id #uuid "dfa4117b-a299-576c-9a40-7e9a99268421" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523228}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "6d226b9a-26b3-5e84-bd16-a03937995368" :question/problem "In what unit direction does the function $f(x,y,z)=\\ln (x^{3}y^{2}z^{4})$ increase most rapidly at the point $(1,1,1)$?" :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $f(x,y,z)$ increases most rapidly in the direction of its gradient.\n\nSo, we compute $∇f(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at the point $(1,1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\ln (x^{3}y^{2}z^{4})),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\ln (x^{3}y^{2}z^{4})),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\ln (x^{3}y^{2}z^{4}))⟩ \\\\ & =⟨\\frac{1}{x^{3}y^{2}z^{4}}\\cdot 3x^{2}y^{2}z^{4},\\,\\frac{1}{x^{3}y^{2}z^{4}}\\cdot 2x^{3}y^{1}z^{4},\\,\\frac{1}{x^{3}y^{2}z^{4}}\\cdot 4x^{3}y^{2}z^{3}⟩ \\\\ & =⟨\\frac{3}{x},\\,\\frac{2}{y},\\,\\frac{4}{z}⟩ \\\\ ∇f(1,1,1) & =⟨\\frac{3}{1},\\,\\frac{2}{1},\\,\\frac{4}{1}⟩ \\\\ & =⟨3,\\,2,\\,4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding unit direction is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =\\frac{∇f(1,1,1)}{‖∇f(1,1,1)‖} \\\\ & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(3)^{2}+(2)^{2}+(4)^{2}}}⟨3,\\,2,\\,4⟩ \\\\ & =⟨\\frac{3 \\sqrt{29}}{29},\\,\\frac{2 \\sqrt{29}}{29},\\,\\frac{4 \\sqrt{29}}{29}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function increases most rapidly in the unit direction of $$\n\\displaystyle ⟨\\frac{3 \\sqrt{29}}{29},\\,\\frac{2 \\sqrt{29}}{29},\\,\\frac{4 \\sqrt{29}}{29}⟩.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523221 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4db28b1e-53e6-5ed7-97fe-dfc2547a1e58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-\\frac{2}{3},\\,-\\frac{1}{3},\\,-\\frac{2}{3}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523205} {:answer/id #uuid "777d568e-614a-5fe8-972d-c790f3d57fd7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{2}{9},\\,\\frac{1}{9},\\,\\frac{2}{9}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523212} {:answer/id #uuid "e7deec0f-97e7-5173-bce8-8cd81ee664db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6},\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6},\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{3}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523230} {:answer/id #uuid "fab2ca73-8b3b-5efe-aa63-5406ad5f0f2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨2,\\,1,\\,2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523233} {:answer/id #uuid "fcf2d532-6987-5e82-abef-4a6d41f98545" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{2}{3},\\,\\frac{1}{3},\\,\\frac{2}{3}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523234}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fcf2d532-6987-5e82-abef-4a6d41f98545" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{2}{3},\\,\\frac{1}{3},\\,\\frac{2}{3}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523234} :answer-field/id #uuid "ce582fa9-bf54-5250-b517-f810eb01ce45" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523225}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "a6e0bfcc-2db2-5134-b63f-ed16edd03447" :question/problem "In what unit direction does the function $f(x,y,z)=\\ln (x^{2}y^{1}z^{6})$ increase most rapidly at the point $(1,1,3)$?" :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $f(x,y,z)$ increases most rapidly in the direction of its gradient.\n\nSo, we compute $∇f(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at the point $(1,1,3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\ln (x^{2}y^{1}z^{6})),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\ln (x^{2}y^{1}z^{6})),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\ln (x^{2}y^{1}z^{6}))⟩ \\\\ & =⟨\\frac{1}{x^{2}y^{1}z^{6}}\\cdot 2x^{1}y^{1}z^{6},\\,\\frac{1}{x^{2}y^{1}z^{6}}\\cdot x^{2}y^{0}z^{6},\\,\\frac{1}{x^{2}y^{1}z^{6}}\\cdot 6x^{2}y^{1}z^{5}⟩ \\\\ & =⟨\\frac{2}{x},\\,\\frac{1}{y},\\,\\frac{6}{z}⟩ \\\\ ∇f(1,1,3) & =⟨\\frac{2}{1},\\,\\frac{1}{1},\\,\\frac{6}{3}⟩ \\\\ & =⟨2,\\,1,\\,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding unit direction is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =\\frac{∇f(1,1,3)}{‖∇f(1,1,3)‖} \\\\ & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(2)^{2}+(1)^{2}+(2)^{2}}}⟨2,\\,1,\\,2⟩ \\\\ & =⟨\\frac{2}{3},\\,\\frac{1}{3},\\,\\frac{2}{3}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function increases most rapidly in the unit direction of $$\n\\displaystyle ⟨\\frac{2}{3},\\,\\frac{1}{3},\\,\\frac{2}{3}⟩.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523222 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4510612c-f80f-5c67-8138-7e4b58f04da5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-\\frac{3}{17},\\,-\\frac{2}{17},\\,-\\frac{2}{17}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523201} {:answer/id #uuid "4d2355f9-7bdf-5219-b0ed-8b759d10a82f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{3 \\sqrt{17}}{17},\\,\\frac{2 \\sqrt{17}}{17},\\,\\frac{2 \\sqrt{17}}{17}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523204} {:answer/id #uuid "8b93f0c7-7cb1-5c58-98b0-702b47f27086" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-\\frac{3 \\sqrt{17}}{17},\\,-\\frac{2 \\sqrt{17}}{17},\\,-\\frac{2 \\sqrt{17}}{17}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523216} {:answer/id #uuid "9d62897b-0837-5ff3-bf6a-1d842243f83d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-\\frac{\\sqrt{33}}{33},\\,-\\frac{4 \\sqrt{33}}{33},\\,-\\frac{4 \\sqrt{33}}{33}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523218} {:answer/id #uuid "e026aebe-c32c-5814-a5e2-37e617185f18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-3,\\,-2,\\,-2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523229}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8b93f0c7-7cb1-5c58-98b0-702b47f27086" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-\\frac{3 \\sqrt{17}}{17},\\,-\\frac{2 \\sqrt{17}}{17},\\,-\\frac{2 \\sqrt{17}}{17}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523216} :answer-field/id #uuid "dc0ce43d-cf21-522b-b594-14d9ac1ad0d8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523227}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "aaff19d2-1089-5ffb-bb3e-92011188d5ba" :question/problem "In what unit direction does the function $f(x,y,z)=\\ln (x^{6}y^{4}z^{4})$ decrease most rapidly at the point $(2,2,2)$?" :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $f(x,y,z)$ decreases most rapidly in the direction opposite to that of its gradient.\n\nSo, we compute $∇f(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at the point $(2,2,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\ln (x^{6}y^{4}z^{4})),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\ln (x^{6}y^{4}z^{4})),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\ln (x^{6}y^{4}z^{4}))⟩ \\\\ & =⟨\\frac{1}{x^{6}y^{4}z^{4}}\\cdot 6x^{5}y^{4}z^{4},\\,\\frac{1}{x^{6}y^{4}z^{4}}\\cdot 4x^{6}y^{3}z^{4},\\,\\frac{1}{x^{6}y^{4}z^{4}}\\cdot 4x^{6}y^{4}z^{3}⟩ \\\\ & =⟨\\frac{6}{x},\\,\\frac{4}{y},\\,\\frac{4}{z}⟩ \\\\ ∇f(2,2,2) & =⟨\\frac{6}{2},\\,\\frac{4}{2},\\,\\frac{4}{2}⟩ \\\\ & =⟨3,\\,2,\\,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding unit direction is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =-\\frac{∇f(2,2,2)}{‖∇f(2,2,2)‖} \\\\ & =\\frac{-1}{\\sqrt{(3)^{2}+(2)^{2}+(2)^{2}}}⟨3,\\,2,\\,2⟩ \\\\ & =⟨-\\frac{3 \\sqrt{17}}{17},\\,-\\frac{2 \\sqrt{17}}{17},\\,-\\frac{2 \\sqrt{17}}{17}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function decreases most rapidly in the unit direction of $$\n\\displaystyle ⟨-\\frac{3 \\sqrt{17}}{17},\\,-\\frac{2 \\sqrt{17}}{17},\\,-\\frac{2 \\sqrt{17}}{17}⟩.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523223 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13eb88b0-c538-59f5-a87f-9f0c62715e7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-\\frac{4}{3},\\,-\\frac{1}{3},\\,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523194} {:answer/id #uuid "24f1dbef-5fc8-59db-bec0-0a9c8a04f080" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{2 \\sqrt{26}}{13},\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{26}}{26},\\,\\frac{3 \\sqrt{26}}{26}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523198} {:answer/id #uuid "8267fe08-c361-5516-bb08-f2287927df19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{11},\\,-\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{11},\\,-\\frac{3 \\sqrt{11}}{11}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523214} {:answer/id #uuid "c20caa2a-1e3e-5e4e-8c04-1a085e20bfe5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-\\frac{2 \\sqrt{26}}{13},\\,-\\frac{\\sqrt{26}}{26},\\,-\\frac{3 \\sqrt{26}}{26}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523224} {:answer/id #uuid "e89ef417-189c-5198-b034-c7b157620d77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-\\frac{6}{13},\\,-\\frac{3}{26},\\,-\\frac{9}{26}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523231}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c20caa2a-1e3e-5e4e-8c04-1a085e20bfe5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-\\frac{2 \\sqrt{26}}{13},\\,-\\frac{\\sqrt{26}}{26},\\,-\\frac{3 \\sqrt{26}}{26}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523224} :answer-field/id #uuid "13f6643a-75d9-50a2-a3fb-88100e3589a1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523195}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "b1292b8d-2b01-5533-896d-f2505b0485b1" :question/problem "In what unit direction does the function $f(x,y,z)=\\ln (x^{4}y^{1}z^{3})$ decrease most rapidly at the point $(3,3,3)$?" :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $f(x,y,z)$ decreases most rapidly in the direction opposite to that of its gradient.\n\nSo, we compute $∇f(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at the point $(3,3,3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\ln (x^{4}y^{1}z^{3})),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\ln (x^{4}y^{1}z^{3})),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\ln (x^{4}y^{1}z^{3}))⟩ \\\\ & =⟨\\frac{1}{x^{4}y^{1}z^{3}}\\cdot 4x^{3}y^{1}z^{3},\\,\\frac{1}{x^{4}y^{1}z^{3}}\\cdot x^{4}y^{0}z^{3},\\,\\frac{1}{x^{4}y^{1}z^{3}}\\cdot 3x^{4}y^{1}z^{2}⟩ \\\\ & =⟨\\frac{4}{x},\\,\\frac{1}{y},\\,\\frac{3}{z}⟩ \\\\ ∇f(3,3,3) & =⟨\\frac{4}{3},\\,\\frac{1}{3},\\,\\frac{3}{3}⟩ \\\\ & =⟨\\frac{4}{3},\\,\\frac{1}{3},\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding unit direction is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =-\\frac{∇f(3,3,3)}{‖∇f(3,3,3)‖} \\\\ & =\\frac{-1}{\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\right)^{2}+\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)^{2}+(1)^{2}}}⟨\\frac{4}{3},\\,\\frac{1}{3},\\,1⟩ \\\\ & =⟨-\\frac{2 \\sqrt{26}}{13},\\,-\\frac{\\sqrt{26}}{26},\\,-\\frac{3 \\sqrt{26}}{26}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function decreases most rapidly in the unit direction of $$\n\\displaystyle ⟨-\\frac{2 \\sqrt{26}}{13},\\,-\\frac{\\sqrt{26}}{26},\\,-\\frac{3 \\sqrt{26}}{26}⟩.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523226 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1be8f1ac-37b5-58a3-955f-0739f5c4af3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-\\frac{5 \\sqrt{43}}{43},\\,-\\frac{3 \\sqrt{43}}{43},\\,-\\frac{3 \\sqrt{43}}{43}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523196} {:answer/id #uuid "24903325-fc0a-5ecb-8f57-b0303e5a47b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{15}{43},\\,\\frac{9}{43},\\,\\frac{9}{43}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523197} {:answer/id #uuid "568e7e09-9ada-5d4d-832e-c907e2ae3641" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{\\sqrt{19}}{19},\\,\\frac{3 \\sqrt{19}}{19},\\,\\frac{3 \\sqrt{19}}{19}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523208} {:answer/id #uuid "58d7dd0e-16b3-5cb7-ae74-c242f146528e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{5 \\sqrt{43}}{43},\\,\\frac{3 \\sqrt{43}}{43},\\,\\frac{3 \\sqrt{43}}{43}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523209} {:answer/id #uuid "8b2f65ba-0cc3-5296-bb1b-f4544ca841ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{5}{3},\\,1,\\,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523215}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "58d7dd0e-16b3-5cb7-ae74-c242f146528e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{5 \\sqrt{43}}{43},\\,\\frac{3 \\sqrt{43}}{43},\\,\\frac{3 \\sqrt{43}}{43}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523209} :answer-field/id #uuid "8c843ff1-9a50-5ae1-8952-8cb69c4ce0ca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523217}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "d24d013a-ecfd-5200-be0e-2480338379cf" :question/problem "In what unit direction does the function $f(x,y,z)=\\ln (x^{5}y^{2}z^{2})$ increase most rapidly at the point $(3,2,2)$?" :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $f(x,y,z)$ increases most rapidly in the direction of its gradient.\n\nSo, we compute $∇f(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at the point $(3,2,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\ln (x^{5}y^{2}z^{2})),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\ln (x^{5}y^{2}z^{2})),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\ln (x^{5}y^{2}z^{2}))⟩ \\\\ & =⟨\\frac{1}{x^{5}y^{2}z^{2}}\\cdot 5x^{4}y^{2}z^{2},\\,\\frac{1}{x^{5}y^{2}z^{2}}\\cdot 2x^{5}y^{1}z^{2},\\,\\frac{1}{x^{5}y^{2}z^{2}}\\cdot 2x^{5}y^{2}z^{1}⟩ \\\\ & =⟨\\frac{5}{x},\\,\\frac{2}{y},\\,\\frac{2}{z}⟩ \\\\ ∇f(3,2,2) & =⟨\\frac{5}{3},\\,\\frac{2}{2},\\,\\frac{2}{2}⟩ \\\\ & =⟨\\frac{5}{3},\\,1,\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding unit direction is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =\\frac{∇f(3,2,2)}{‖∇f(3,2,2)‖} \\\\ & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{5}{3}\\right)^{2}+(1)^{2}+(1)^{2}}}⟨\\frac{5}{3},\\,1,\\,1⟩ \\\\ & =⟨\\frac{5 \\sqrt{43}}{43},\\,\\frac{3 \\sqrt{43}}{43},\\,\\frac{3 \\sqrt{43}}{43}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function increases most rapidly in the unit direction of $$\n\\displaystyle ⟨\\frac{5 \\sqrt{43}}{43},\\,\\frac{3 \\sqrt{43}}{43},\\,\\frac{3 \\sqrt{43}}{43}⟩.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548195 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0819b00b-ddf8-51fd-b337-f67bbb68e05a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{24 \\sqrt{5}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548189} {:answer/id #uuid "2d2d0946-fae9-532f-80d5-010b263a3d4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4 \\sqrt{5}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548190} {:answer/id #uuid "66c7d9c2-81ae-5068-8c96-91f51d97a8ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{24}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548191} {:answer/id #uuid "895ba611-f922-55ef-9624-62e08b1e4ef5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{16 \\sqrt{5}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548193} {:answer/id #uuid "c4eef04d-da15-5f2a-aac3-6cfce868c8fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548194}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0819b00b-ddf8-51fd-b337-f67bbb68e05a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{24 \\sqrt{5}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548189} :answer-field/id #uuid "70e128fd-ce69-5958-8d26-3af81c7ff0b1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548192}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "e4d2c788-fb3a-5910-a90d-014c2de97596" :question/problem "Find the directional derivative of the function $f(x,y)=4 x^{2} y + 2 x e^{y}$ at the point $A(2,0)$ in the direction towards the point $B(4,1).$" :question/worked-solution "The directional derivative ${f}_{u}^{′}(a,b)$ of a function $f(x,y)$ at the point $(a,b)$ in the direction of the vector $v$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{u}^{′}(a,b)=∇f(a,b)\\cdot u\n$$\n\nwhere $u$ is a unit vector in the direction of $v.$\n\nFirst, we find the direction vector and the corresponding unit vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =⟨4,1⟩-⟨2,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,1⟩ \\\\ u & =\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{AB}}{‖\\overset{\\to }{AB}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(2)^{2}+(1)^{2}}}⟨2,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}⟨2,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the gradient $∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $A(2,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4 x^{2} y + 2 x e^{y}),\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4 x^{2} y + 2 x e^{y})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨8 x y + 2 e^{y},2 x \\left(2 x + e^{y}\\right)⟩ \\\\ ∇f(2,0) & =⟨8 (2) (0) + 2 e^{(0)},4 (2)^{2} + 2 (2) e^{(0)}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,20⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the directional derivative is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{u}^{′}(2,0) & =∇f(2,0)\\cdot u \\\\  & =⟨2,20⟩\\cdot ⟨\\frac{2 \\sqrt{5}}{5},\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{24 \\sqrt{5}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563650 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5aa574d4-0537-5c0c-bc48-fbc415d1fec3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2e+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563148} {:answer/id #uuid "5c6bd500-a363-5876-8fc0-e3190803b613" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563153} {:answer/id #uuid "605a4f94-5783-5014-8c0c-c25bbcb39d7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4e-3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563167} {:answer/id #uuid "83f43442-0622-5df7-8058-787b935ebf97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563240} {:answer/id #uuid "e79adf25-7969-5327-8be1-24d409ff0b06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563461}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "605a4f94-5783-5014-8c0c-c25bbcb39d7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4e-3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563167} :answer-field/id #uuid "75da4e92-2341-5f89-a517-37e979e4303c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563575}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "0fb365d6-3918-560a-af36-ae398310b595" :question/math-academy-id "q-130802" :question/problem "Consider the function $g(x,y)={y}^{x}.$ What is the rate of change of $g(x,y)$ at the point $(1,e)$ in the direction of the vector $v=⟨4,-3⟩?$" :question/worked-solution "The rate of change of $g(x,y)$ at the point $(1,e)$ in the direction of $v$ is equal to the directional derivative ${g}_{u}^{′}(1,e).$ \n\nFirst, we find the unit vector $u$ in the direction of $v:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖ & =\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+(-3)^{2}}=5 \\\\ u & =\\frac{1}{5}⟨4,-3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the gradient $∇g(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(1,e):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇g(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{x}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{x})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{y}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y,\\,x{y}^{x-1}⟩ \\\\ ∇g(1,e) & =⟨{e}^{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡e,1{e}^{1-1}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨e,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the directional derivative ${g}_{u}^{′}(1,e)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}_{u}^{′}(1,e) & =∇g(1,e)\\cdot u \\\\  & =⟨e,1⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}⟨4,-3⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{e(4)+1(-3)}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{4e-3}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563689 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "189ec891-a762-54dd-8e28-66f7d86f7621" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563000} {:answer/id #uuid "a937d5cd-9309-5b00-a446-cdfd649551b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563326} {:answer/id #uuid "b2d80ddb-b0ea-50ab-81d2-6892d56e013c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563340} {:answer/id #uuid "cd1cc6cd-5009-53a5-9550-f7f07275d8a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563398} {:answer/id #uuid "e69a9ba3-ccae-56d8-8e5f-5d06921f70e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563452}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cd1cc6cd-5009-53a5-9550-f7f07275d8a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563398} :answer-field/id #uuid "45ab83aa-a438-50c5-87d1-85ddbe0b15e9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563560}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "56172e38-6320-5f34-8b75-aab1067f37e6" :question/math-academy-id "q-86543" :question/problem "Consider the function $g(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x).$ What is the rate of change of $g(x,y)$ at the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{4},4)$ in the direction in which the function increases most rapidly?" :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $g(x,y)$ increases most rapidly in the direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nIn our case, we compute $∇g(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{4},4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,g(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\operatorname{tan}⁡x,\\,\\frac{2}{y}⟩ \\\\ ∇\\,g(\\frac{\\pi }{4},4) & =⟨-\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right),\\,\\frac{2}{4}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,\\,\\frac{1}{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the rate of change of $g(x,y)$ at the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{4},4)$ in the direction of $∇g(\\frac{\\pi }{4},4)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖∇\\,g(\\frac{\\pi }{4},4)‖ & =‖⟨-1,\\frac{1}{2}⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{4}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{5}{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563713 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "053be929-7775-5fb0-bbbd-3704056bc94e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,0,-2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562948} {:answer/id #uuid "1e39c336-a544-5c66-93cf-3ffb2d7f86bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,2,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563011} {:answer/id #uuid "352ad72c-95f5-5fae-a15a-05da0c1da3cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-1,-1,2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563065} {:answer/id #uuid "53343afc-152e-5acb-9d54-08cb170116b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,-2,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563133} {:answer/id #uuid "c90851b6-ccde-53db-beb3-491291ef5d9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨2,2,-4⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563388}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c90851b6-ccde-53db-beb3-491291ef5d9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨2,2,-4⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563388} :answer-field/id #uuid "ff3c854a-2ec4-5aac-9265-718757aee35e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563642}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "8889b56a-bb69-56d7-b6a6-42f214a1a3f7" :question/math-academy-id "q-130270" :question/problem "In what direction does the function $g(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}-xyz$ increase most rapidly at the point $(1,0,-2)?$" :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $g(x,y,z)$ increases most rapidly in the direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nSo, we compute $∇g(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at the point $(1,0,-2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇g(x,y,z) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}-xyz),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}-xyz),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}-xyz)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2x-yz,\\,2y-xz,\\,2z-xy⟩ \\\\ ∇g(1,0,-2) & =⟨2(1)-0(-2),2(0)-1(-2),2(-2)-1(0)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,2,-4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function increases most rapidly in the direction of $⟨2,2,-4⟩.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563741 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00433fb2-2689-50b6-b5ae-b3213b484e7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562937} {:answer/id #uuid "81f0d8bc-19f0-5e3d-ad6f-8e1c66829afd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563232} {:answer/id #uuid "bc4239ca-8453-592c-a801-dc055bf9ad37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563360} {:answer/id #uuid "c97f5424-984d-5602-8981-349f5455aec9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563389} {:answer/id #uuid "da9987fb-7331-55ac-8916-60a446822552" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563423}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "da9987fb-7331-55ac-8916-60a446822552" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563423} :answer-field/id #uuid "aa2be9cd-8e25-5c22-a57c-dbf36efef798" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563612}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d5075c32-f624-572f-a2d3-78616466d14e" :question/math-academy-id "q-86430" :question/problem "Find the directional derivative of the function $f(x,y)=(2{x}^{2}-y)^{2}$ at the point $A(-1,1)$ in the direction towards the point $B(-2,2).$" :question/worked-solution "The directional derivative ${f}_{u}^{′}(a,b)$ of a function $f(x,y)$ at the point $(a,b)$ in the direction of the vector $v$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{u}^{′}(a,b)=∇f(a,b)\\cdot u\n$$\n\nwhere $u$ is a unit vector in the direction of $v.$\n\nFirst, we find the direction vector and the corresponding unit vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =⟨-2,2⟩-⟨-1,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,1⟩ \\\\ u & =\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{AB}}{‖\\overset{\\to }{AB}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}}}⟨-1,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨-1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the gradient $∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $A(-1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{x}^{2}-y)^{2},\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{x}^{2}-y)^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨16{x}^{3}-8xy,\\,2y-4{x}^{2}⟩ \\\\ ∇f(-1,1) & =⟨16(-1)^{3}-8(-1)(1),\\,2(1)-4(-1)^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-8,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the directional derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{u}^{′}(-1,1) & =∇f(-1,1)\\cdot u \\\\  & =⟨-8,-2⟩\\cdot ⟨-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}},\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟩ \\\\  & =(-8)\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\right)-2\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563750 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04dcdfb7-813c-5013-92a6-1f6a2e40d34a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562947} {:answer/id #uuid "3293ed3c-f84a-5bdd-b26a-c1ac19d7d55f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563059} {:answer/id #uuid "6635b13b-ede2-5436-984d-23828cf04773" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563181} {:answer/id #uuid "7d25f5da-9280-508e-a834-dd75fe932211" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563220} {:answer/id #uuid "992d4136-ab07-5324-b78a-7177af5b18ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563290}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3293ed3c-f84a-5bdd-b26a-c1ac19d7d55f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563059} :answer-field/id #uuid "235850d9-4b74-5c62-9d1d-41026c4c9828" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563542}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "df6440e4-6b40-5b4e-87c9-62e58eb89d0e" :question/math-academy-id "q-130258" :question/problem "Find the directional derivative of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-6xy+{y}^{2}$ at the point $(-1,3)$ in the direction of the vector $v=i+j.$" :question/worked-solution "The directional derivative ${f}_{u}^{′}(a,b)$ of a function $f(x,y)$ at the point $(a,b)$ in the direction of the vector $v$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{u}^{′}(a,b)=∇f(a,b)\\cdot u\n$$\n\nwhere $u$ is a unit vector in the direction of $v.$\n\nFirst, we find the unit vector $u$ in the direction of $v:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖ & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}=\\sqrt{2} \\\\ u & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(i+j)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the gradient $∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(-1,3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-6xy+{y}^{2})\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-6xy+{y}^{2})\\text{ }j \\\\  & =(2x-6y)\\,i+(2y-6x)j \\\\ ∇\\,f(-1,3) & =(2(-1)-6(3))\\,i+(2(3)-6(-1))j \\\\  & =-20\\,i+12\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the directional derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{u}^{′}(-1,3) & =∇\\,f(-1,3)\\cdot u \\\\  & =(-20\\,i+12\\,j)\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(i+j) \\\\  & =\\frac{-20(1)+12(1)}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =-4\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563760 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c8383c9-5a00-5106-8ade-ebcb1afc1644" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562966} {:answer/id #uuid "2ea52a0e-e6a5-522f-8a2f-e28cb7fa9518" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563050} {:answer/id #uuid "48b88652-e6ed-5cdf-94e0-9c52630df3e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563105} {:answer/id #uuid "68b0f87b-d56a-5e56-bcb2-6c135e0e44a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563187} {:answer/id #uuid "804a3d7f-93d1-5460-9e8a-4cea93ac58d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563227}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2ea52a0e-e6a5-522f-8a2f-e28cb7fa9518" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563050} :answer-field/id #uuid "5f6b8a55-8b0f-5157-98ff-b7ec894bdaee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563567}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f7872fd6-9c54-5e48-b08b-71d8ca0a2063" :question/math-academy-id "q-83552" :question/problem "Find the directional derivative of the function $f(x,y)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ at the point $(1,1)$ in the direction of the vector $v=2i+j.$" :question/worked-solution "The directional derivative ${f}_{u}^{′}(a,b)$ of a function $f(x,y)$ at the point $(a,b)$ in the direction of the vector $v$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{u}^{′}(a,b)=∇f(a,b)\\cdot u\n$$\n\nwhere $u$ is a unit vector in the direction of $v.$\n\nFirst, we find the unit vector $u$ in the direction of $v:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖ & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}}=\\sqrt{5} \\\\ u & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}(2i+j)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the gradient $∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)\\text{ }j \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+\\frac{y}{x})\\,i+(\\frac{x}{y}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)\\,j \\\\ ∇\\,f(1,1) & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡1+\\frac{1}{1})\\,i+(\\frac{1}{1}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡1)\\,j \\\\  & =i+j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the directional derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{u}^{′}(1,1) & =∇\\,f(1,1)\\cdot u \\\\  & =(i+j)\\cdot (\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}i+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}j) \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot 2+1\\cdot 1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563763 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "57ea209f-e155-526a-94ca-0d966c493e42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\,i+\\frac{1}{4}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563141} {:answer/id #uuid "96c6d7cb-49e7-5e45-b566-508c8a442dec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4\\,i-2\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563281} {:answer/id #uuid "9f5e6c1e-a123-5805-9a48-ff55d4732a4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6\\,i-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563304} {:answer/id #uuid "b3791795-25ea-52db-9a06-ea2ad48e2ced" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\,i-\\frac{1}{2}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563341} {:answer/id #uuid "d18ea431-dd2b-59ac-94dc-2a47eca46a91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4\\,i-\\frac{1}{2}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563406}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "96c6d7cb-49e7-5e45-b566-508c8a442dec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4\\,i-2\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563281} :answer-field/id #uuid "fe69802c-24d6-5c9a-b43c-e07a25e5de2d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563641}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ffacb628-f280-51e0-9811-c0f525cc08ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-83591" :question/problem "In what direction does the function $f(x,y)=x\\operatorname{tan}⁡y+2{x}^{2}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ decrease most rapidly at the point $(1,\\frac{\\pi }{4})?$" :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $f(x,y)$ decreases most rapidly in the direction opposite to that of its gradient.\n\nSo, we compute $-∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(1,\\frac{\\pi }{4}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-∇f(x,y) & =-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x\\operatorname{tan}⁡y+2{x}^{2}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)\\text{ }i-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x\\operatorname{tan}⁡y+2{x}^{2}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)\\text{ }j \\\\  & =-(\\operatorname{tan}⁡y+4x-\\frac{1}{x})i-x{sec}^{2}⁡y\\text{ }j \\\\ -∇f(1,\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =-(\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)+4\\cdot 1-\\frac{1}{1})i-1\\cdot {sec}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)\\text{ }j \\\\  & =-4\\,i-2\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function decreases most rapidly in the direction of $-4\\,i-2\\,j.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186653564 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04d46f58-6728-5d7e-af77-e0a58bbd497a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186653091} {:answer/id #uuid "72fac567-96ad-5cec-a3ac-c6e988127006" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186653248} {:answer/id #uuid "76c5d9e8-bbde-5c77-a47f-279b3794c0f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186653253} {:answer/id #uuid "a3fc4a80-b819-5043-a846-0e7d55902058" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186653308} {:answer/id #uuid "fa361707-efb1-59da-a591-2a5a003acd22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186653439}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a3fc4a80-b819-5043-a846-0e7d55902058" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186653308} :answer-field/id #uuid "0972614c-21d4-598f-8522-bcb2bc2b9874" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186653446}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "6ef219b5-1e6d-577f-990a-3bfd77335bcb" :question/math-academy-id "q-130267" :question/problem "Find the directional derivative of the function $f(x,y)=2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y$ at the point $A(1,e)$ in the direction towards the point $B(-2,e).$" :question/worked-solution "The directional derivative ${f}_{u}^{′}(a,b)$ of a function $f(x,y)$ at the point $(a,b)$ in the direction of the vector $v$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{u}^{′}(a,b)=∇f(a,b)\\cdot u\n$$\n\nwhere $u$ is a unit vector in the direction of $v.$\n\nFirst, we find the direction vector and the corresponding unit vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =⟨-2,e⟩-⟨1,e⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-3,0⟩ \\\\ u & =\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{AB}}{‖\\overset{\\to }{AB}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+{0}^{2}}}⟨-3,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨-3,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the gradient $∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $A(1,e):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2\\operatorname{ln}⁡y,\\,\\frac{2x}{y}⟩ \\\\ ∇f(1,e) & =⟨2\\operatorname{ln}⁡e,\\,\\frac{2(1)}{e}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,\\frac{2}{e}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the directional derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{u}^{′}(1,e) & =∇f(1,e)\\cdot u \\\\  & =⟨2,\\frac{2}{e}⟩\\cdot ⟨-1,0⟩ \\\\  & =2(-1)+\\frac{2}{e}(0) \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186653567 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a0533b5-c2e7-511c-9f05-15e62d822595" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\,i+\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186653174} {:answer/id #uuid "5ea7f147-abd6-55d2-9e7c-d8f78237c3a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186653224} {:answer/id #uuid "8a9c4991-385c-5469-bb1f-2fd3e88f2d93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i-j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186653276} {:answer/id #uuid "a7368579-64e1-5463-8f79-87ec8f422b0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,i-3\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186653316} {:answer/id #uuid "b11eb11a-848f-50df-8a50-83dd9957c1ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186653332}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5ea7f147-abd6-55d2-9e7c-d8f78237c3a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186653224} :answer-field/id #uuid "28c32306-6d99-5b8b-a4e2-b4d7c370948d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186653457}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "8328d523-659d-5c19-b613-eb105d51a8f5" :question/math-academy-id "q-130278" :question/problem "In what direction does the function $f(x,y)={x}^{y}$ increase most rapidly at the point $(2,1)?$" :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $f(x,y)$ increases most rapidly in the direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nSo, we compute $∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(2,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{y})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{y})\\,j \\\\  & =y{x}^{y-1}\\,i+{x}^{y}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,j \\\\ ∇f(2,1) & =(1){2}^{1-1}\\,i+({2}^{1})\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\,j \\\\  & =i+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function increases most rapidly in the direction of $i+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\,j.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186653594 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17e09453-1970-5cf4-8a28-b7c837d3e3eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186653111} {:answer/id #uuid "4f7f4fc2-33e4-5443-a332-4cba535cf443" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186653208} {:answer/id #uuid "5bb4d111-123b-5f5b-8aeb-e3dea9bdbde5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186653219} {:answer/id #uuid "6d2685d3-aa4b-541f-b039-4afaf4357429" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186653237} {:answer/id #uuid "b53745bd-45b1-5bbb-8bd5-72c703e722d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186653335}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5bb4d111-123b-5f5b-8aeb-e3dea9bdbde5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186653219} :answer-field/id #uuid "3a820d2b-204b-588a-97ad-656d06c18308" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186653463}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "f3874b3a-6613-53ba-8cc9-34e3e77013db" :question/math-academy-id "q-130810" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}{y}^{3}.$ What is the rate of change of $f(x,y)$ at the point $(2,-1)$ in the direction in which the function decreases most rapidly?" :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $f(x,y)$ decreases most rapidly in the opposite direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nIn our case, we compute $-∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(2,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-∇f(x,y) & =-\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}{y}^{3})i-\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}{y}^{3})j \\\\  & =-2x{y}^{3}\\,i-3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,j \\\\ -∇f(2,-1) & =-2(2)(-1)^{3}\\,i-3(2)^{2}(-1)^{2}j \\\\  & =4\\,i-12\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the rate of change of $f(x,y)$ at the point $(2,-1)$ in the direction of $-∇f(2,-1)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖-∇f(2,-1)‖ & =‖4\\,i-12\\,j‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+(-12)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{160} \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186725116 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "49a2c2f3-d8a8-5d8a-8fb7-c07f95e2fa18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186725107} {:answer/id #uuid "5027ab94-0251-551e-89f5-7fa970132140" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186725108} {:answer/id #uuid "5036ff67-7bdc-56ea-a39e-86726cc0abd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186725109} {:answer/id #uuid "5254616d-9668-503f-bb2e-845c94341c9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{28}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186725111} {:answer/id #uuid "b1c28309-ca10-54c7-b644-8a8c48916fc6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186725114}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5036ff67-7bdc-56ea-a39e-86726cc0abd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186725109} :answer-field/id #uuid "eed5dc07-fb31-5332-8176-261ba7d2a2ec" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186725119}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c296adb0-2561-528e-9e42-a549b25c773d" :question/problem "Find the directional derivative of the function $g(x,y)=(x^2+y)^2$ at the point $A(1,-2)$ in the direction towards the point $B(-2,2)$." :question/worked-solution "The directional derivative at a point $A$ in the direction of a nonzero vector $\\mathbf v$ is\n\n$$\nD_{\\mathbf u}g(A)=\\nabla g(A)\\cdot\\mathbf u,\n\\qquad \\mathbf u=\\frac{\\mathbf v}{\\|\\mathbf v\\|}.\n$$\n\nThe direction must be represented by a unit vector.\n\nThe direction is from $A$ towards $B$, so subtract the coordinates in that order and normalize:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf v&=\\overrightarrow{AB}=\\langle -2-1,2-(-2)\\rangle=\\langle -3,4\\rangle,\\\\\n\\|\\mathbf v\\|&=\\sqrt{(-3)^2+4^2}=5,\\\\\n\\mathbf u&=\\left\\langle -\\frac{3}{5},\\frac{4}{5}\\right\\rangle.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApply the chain rule to both partial derivatives, then evaluate at $A$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng_x(x,y)&=2(x^2+y)(2x)=4x(x^2+y),\\\\\ng_y(x,y)&=2(x^2+y),\\\\\n\\nabla g(1,-2)&=\\langle 4(1)(1-2),2(1-2)\\rangle\\\\\n&=\\langle -4,-2\\rangle.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nD_{\\mathbf u}g(1,-2)&=\\langle -4,-2\\rangle\\cdot\\left\\langle -\\frac{3}{5},\\frac{4}{5}\\right\\rangle\\\\\n&=\\frac{(-4)(-3)+(-2)(4)}{5}\\\\\n&=\\frac{12-8}{5}=\\frac{4}{5}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186725117 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0afc0811-7274-57fb-a09d-67c8f70199a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186725104} {:answer/id #uuid "7133a94d-8fea-58d8-a15b-c945b0cdf20a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186725112} {:answer/id #uuid "7d6f4edf-5123-5d48-96ad-10cd27b144ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186725113} {:answer/id #uuid "bae7b90b-5d8c-5377-b195-26724808f7be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{14\\sqrt{5}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186725115} {:answer/id #uuid "fafb5ad3-faa4-5e50-838b-4b665f79bece" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186725120}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7133a94d-8fea-58d8-a15b-c945b0cdf20a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186725112} :answer-field/id #uuid "2f25c80d-3f1f-5a9b-a169-383d56430fe9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186725106}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d61f7599-aa77-51db-b099-2ccf964dc7ff" :question/problem "Find the directional derivative of the function $f(x,y)=x^2+2xy$ at the point $(1,2)$ in the direction of the vector $\\mathbf v=2\\mathbf i-\\mathbf j$." :question/worked-solution "The directional derivative at a point $A$ in the direction of a nonzero vector $\\mathbf v$ is\n\n$$\nD_{\\mathbf u}f(A)=\\nabla f(A)\\cdot\\mathbf u,\n\\qquad \\mathbf u=\\frac{\\mathbf v}{\\|\\mathbf v\\|}.\n$$\n\nThe direction must be represented by a unit vector.\n\nFirst, normalize the supplied direction vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\|\\mathbf v\\|&=\\sqrt{2^2+(-1)^2}=\\sqrt{5},\\\\\n\\mathbf u&=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\langle 2,-1\\rangle.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow compute the partial derivatives and evaluate the gradient at $(1,2)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x(x,y)&=2x+2y,\\\\\nf_y(x,y)&=2x,\\\\\n\\nabla f(1,2)&=\\langle 2(1)+2(2),2(1)\\rangle=\\langle 6,2\\rangle.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTaking the dot product gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nD_{\\mathbf u}f(1,2)&=\\langle 6,2\\rangle\\cdot\\frac{\\langle 2,-1\\rangle}{\\sqrt{5}}\\\\\n&=\\frac{6(2)+2(-1)}{\\sqrt{5}}\\\\\n&=\\frac{10}{\\sqrt{5}}=2\\sqrt{5}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186725118 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2efddd40-658d-5fa2-8f0a-037d5f305c8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15\\sqrt{13}}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186725105}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2efddd40-658d-5fa2-8f0a-037d5f305c8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15\\sqrt{13}}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186725105} :answer-field/id #uuid "5079543a-73a4-522d-806d-3d04ff473efb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186725110}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "d8cdb080-bad0-522b-9483-8dec1e7ba322" :question/problem "Find the directional derivative of the function $h(x,y)=\\dfrac{x^2+y}{x-y}$ at the point $A(2,1)$ in the direction towards the point $B(-1,3)$.\n\nThe required directional derivative is {{field-1}}. Give an exact value." :question/worked-solution "The directional derivative at a point $A$ in the direction of a nonzero vector $\\mathbf v$ is\n\n$$\nD_{\\mathbf u}h(A)=\\nabla h(A)\\cdot\\mathbf u,\n\\qquad \\mathbf u=\\frac{\\mathbf v}{\\|\\mathbf v\\|}.\n$$\n\nThe direction must be represented by a unit vector.\n\nFirst find the direction from $A$ towards $B$ and its unit vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf v&=\\overrightarrow{AB}=\\langle -1-2,3-1\\rangle=\\langle -3,2\\rangle,\\\\\n\\|\\mathbf v\\|&=\\sqrt{(-3)^2+2^2}=\\sqrt{13},\\\\\n\\mathbf u&=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}\\langle -3,2\\rangle.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $2-1=1\\ne 0$, the function is differentiable near $A$. Use the quotient rule for each partial derivative. In the $y$ derivative, the derivative of the denominator $x-y$ is $-1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nh_x(x,y)&=\\frac{2x(x-y)-(x^2+y)}{(x-y)^2},\\\\\nh_y(x,y)&=\\frac{(x-y)+(x^2+y)}{(x-y)^2},\\\\\nh_x(2,1)&=\\frac{4(1)-5}{1^2}=-1,\\\\\nh_y(2,1)&=\\frac{1+5}{1^2}=6.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluate the gradient at the starting point $A$, then take the dot product with the unit direction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nD_{\\mathbf u}h(2,1)&=\\langle -1,6\\rangle\\cdot\\frac{\\langle -3,2\\rangle}{\\sqrt{13}}\\\\\n&=\\frac{(-1)(-3)+6(2)}{\\sqrt{13}}\\\\\n&=\\frac{15}{\\sqrt{13}}=\\frac{15\\sqrt{13}}{13}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186725149 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30c71e1e-fd84-5c3b-b421-e3e667dd9f1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\langle -3,-4,-1\\rangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186725150} {:answer/id #uuid "3fc7c0f5-797a-5799-909f-eccc1bba26f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\langle -3,-4,1\\rangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186725151} {:answer/id #uuid "74627ad5-18b1-57f5-8ac5-96654f6709c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\langle -3,4,1\\rangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186725155} {:answer/id #uuid "8a84de8a-d854-5515-b354-6fd752f6c804" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\langle 3,4,-1\\rangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186725156} {:answer/id #uuid "b8e16bd8-19d1-554b-85c2-c012cf64fc92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\langle -4,-4,0\\rangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186725165}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3fc7c0f5-797a-5799-909f-eccc1bba26f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\langle -3,-4,1\\rangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186725151} :answer-field/id #uuid "0de94b4e-2070-5e4c-b4dd-2f95cecd0411" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186725148}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "2b4fa4f0-f229-5148-b655-849a7fb3e480" :question/problem "In what direction does the function $g(x,y,z)=xy^2-3\\ln(x+z)$ decrease most rapidly at the point $(1,2,2)$?" :question/worked-solution "The point is in the domain because $x+z=3>0$. A differentiable function decreases most rapidly in the direction opposite to its gradient.\n\nCompute all three partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng_x&=y^2-\\frac{3}{x+z},\\\\\ng_y&=2xy,\\\\\ng_z&=-\\frac{3}{x+z}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe logarithm contributes to the $x$ and $z$ components, but not the $y$ component. At the given point,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\nabla g(1,2,2)&=\\left\\langle 2^2-\\frac{3}{1+2},\\,2(1)(2),\\,-\\frac{3}{1+2}\\right\\rangle\\\\\n&=\\langle 3,4,-1\\rangle.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReverse every component to obtain the direction of fastest decrease:\n\n$$\n-\\nabla g(1,2,2)=\\langle -3,-4,1\\rangle.\n$$\n\nAny positive multiple of this vector represents the same direction."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186725153 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02e71be4-ef30-5173-bfd9-9c13376ef8a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\mathbf{i}+3\\mathbf{j}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186725147} {:answer/id #uuid "8e516e50-6c84-5490-8db6-57461c5942c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\mathbf{i}+5\\mathbf{j}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186725157} {:answer/id #uuid "b0b1752d-4e0c-504e-bd1e-ee59e9dd9fef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\mathbf{i}-3\\mathbf{j}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186725161} {:answer/id #uuid "b69d795e-c264-581f-8ace-c558171859ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186725164} {:answer/id #uuid "f8929736-e145-549a-b26e-204b361e5b06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\mathbf{i}-4\\mathbf{j}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186725167}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b0b1752d-4e0c-504e-bd1e-ee59e9dd9fef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\mathbf{i}-3\\mathbf{j}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186725161} :answer-field/id #uuid "b3fe31ff-22bc-504a-8c49-c90cf972cbb9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186725163}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "620dd0c9-8c6e-5b6e-9cdc-c7d542686ca2" :question/problem "In what direction does the function $f(x,y)=x^2+xy+2y^2$ increase most rapidly at the point $(1,-1)$?" :question/worked-solution "A differentiable function increases most rapidly in the direction of its gradient.\n\nCompute the partial derivatives while holding the other variable constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x(x,y)&=2x+y,\\\\\nf_y(x,y)&=x+4y.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluate at the given point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\nabla f(1,-1)&=(2(1)+(-1))\\mathbf{i}+(1+4(-1))\\mathbf{j}\\\\\n&=\\mathbf{i}-3\\mathbf{j}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus $f$ increases most rapidly in the direction of $\\mathbf{i}-3\\mathbf{j}$. A direction vector need not have unit length; any positive multiple points in the same direction."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186725158 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "719e70e0-c7ca-5815-8305-8f42255e8a9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\langle \\frac72,-1,\\frac52\\right\\rangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186725154} {:answer/id #uuid "ae945156-b910-5e20-b206-7f79605bf706" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\langle -3,1,-2\\rangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186725159} {:answer/id #uuid "aebd2411-8686-5770-b4ee-190fc6f57eed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\langle -\\frac72,1,-\\frac52\\right\\rangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186725160} {:answer/id #uuid "b1628b1c-30ef-5153-996e-8ae4bece4762" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\langle -\\frac72,-3,-\\frac52\\right\\rangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186725162} {:answer/id #uuid "d2668fa4-0fe0-54eb-afe2-a9c92a152f95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\langle -\\frac52,2,-\\frac52\\right\\rangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186725166}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aebd2411-8686-5770-b4ee-190fc6f57eed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\langle -\\frac72,1,-\\frac52\\right\\rangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186725160} :answer-field/id #uuid "4c610583-202b-571d-a032-66487e0e2629" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186725152}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "946fa2e2-881f-571b-9419-6cb29d1ee9c5" :question/problem "In what direction does the function $h(x,y,z)=(x+y)e^{xz-y}+\\ln(x+z)$ decrease most rapidly at the point $(1,1,1)$?" :question/worked-solution "The point is in the domain because $x+z=2>0$. The direction of fastest decrease is opposite to the gradient.\n\nDifferentiate before substituting the point. The product rule is needed for the $x$ and $y$ partial derivatives, and the exponential requires the chain rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nh_x&=e^{xz-y}+(x+y)z e^{xz-y}+\\frac{1}{x+z},\\\\\nh_y&=e^{xz-y}-(x+y)e^{xz-y},\\\\\nh_z&=(x+y)x e^{xz-y}+\\frac{1}{x+z}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the $y$ partial derivative, the minus sign comes from $\\partial(xz-y)/\\partial y=-1$. The logarithm contributes only to the $x$ and $z$ partial derivatives.\n\nAt $(1,1,1)$, the exponential is $e^{1(1)-1}=1$ and $x+y=x+z=2$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\nabla h(1,1,1)&=\\left\\langle 1+2(1)+\\frac12,\\,1-2,\\,2(1)+\\frac12\\right\\rangle\\\\\n&=\\left\\langle \\frac72,-1,\\frac52\\right\\rangle.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNegate all three components:\n\n$$\n-\\nabla h(1,1,1)=\\left\\langle -\\frac72,1,-\\frac52\\right\\rangle.\n$$\n\nThus $h$ decreases most rapidly in this direction. Multiplying this vector by a positive number does not change its direction."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186725176 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3d0ef2f8-083a-50ec-bf3c-90165f6a0af1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{34}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186725173} {:answer/id #uuid "4e1f644b-3807-5402-8928-5cb7bb96b1d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{34}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186725174} {:answer/id #uuid "a9ea83b0-beb2-5760-9afd-03749485b3da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186725179} {:answer/id #uuid "cd3cf800-3255-56e4-9789-09f2e5c3221d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "34" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186725187} {:answer/id #uuid "f252eec4-c396-57ed-b71e-f075cc963779" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186725189}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3d0ef2f8-083a-50ec-bf3c-90165f6a0af1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{34}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186725173} :answer-field/id #uuid "1594e010-d986-53a2-8184-c62120caed99" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186725170}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "7c3872a4-b205-5c7c-b703-46095429c02b" :question/problem "Consider the function $g(x,y)=2x^2-3y^2$. What is the rate of change of $g$ at the point $(1,1)$ in the direction of the vector $\\mathbf v=\\langle 4,-3\\rangle$?" :question/worked-solution "The rate of change is the directional derivative $D_{\\mathbf u}g(1,1)$, where $\\mathbf u$ is the unit vector in the direction of $\\mathbf v$.\n\nFirst normalize the direction vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\|\\mathbf v\\|&=\\sqrt{4^2+(-3)^2}=5,\\\\\n\\mathbf u&=\\left\\langle\\frac45,-\\frac35\\right\\rangle.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext compute the gradient and evaluate it at the given point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\nabla g(x,y)&=\\langle 4x,-6y\\rangle,\\\\\n\\nabla g(1,1)&=\\langle 4,-6\\rangle.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally take the dot product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nD_{\\mathbf u}g(1,1)&=\\nabla g(1,1)\\cdot\\mathbf u\\\\\n&=4\\left(\\frac45\\right)+(-6)\\left(-\\frac35\\right)\\\\\n&=\\frac{16+18}{5}=\\frac{34}{5}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe positive sign means that $g$ increases in this direction."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186725180 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3ac76a86-3bf9-5b38-98c9-e003e396d08b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2(3)+(-1)(4)}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186725172} {:answer/id #uuid "6fcfd47d-1e69-5889-94e3-56f10a424d27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2(3)+(-1)(4)}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186725175} {:answer/id #uuid "bb0c1ba2-4f49-54e1-b2a5-0f5471940549" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2(3)+(-1)(4)}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186725182} {:answer/id #uuid "bcd1d0b4-422e-5180-a86f-cabb13268abd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2^2+(-1)^2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186725183} {:answer/id #uuid "c62e63ac-f7f2-553d-9c1f-cdc2f94c9747" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(3)+(-1)(4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186725185}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6fcfd47d-1e69-5889-94e3-56f10a424d27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2(3)+(-1)(4)}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186725175} :answer-field/id #uuid "1576970c-a42c-5586-a887-b264d9493c28" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186725169}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b4ebc991-e7e9-5861-a8d4-86aed8a656fc" :question/problem "At a point $P$, a differentiable function $f(x,y)$ has gradient $\\nabla f(P)=\\langle 2,-1\\rangle$.\n\nWhich expression gives the rate of change of $f$ at $P$ in the direction of $\\mathbf v=\\langle 3,4\\rangle$? You do not need to evaluate the expression." :question/worked-solution "The rate of change in a direction is the directional derivative\n\n$$\nD_{\\mathbf u}f(P)=\\nabla f(P)\\cdot\\mathbf u,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf u$ is a unit vector in the specified direction. Since $\\|\\mathbf v\\|=\\sqrt{3^2+4^2}=5$,\n\n$$\n\\mathbf u=\\frac{\\mathbf v}{\\|\\mathbf v\\|}=\\left\\langle\\frac35,\\frac45\\right\\rangle.\n$$\n\nThus the required expression is\n\n$$\n\\langle 2,-1\\rangle\\cdot\\left\\langle\\frac35,\\frac45\\right\\rangle\n=\\frac{2(3)+(-1)(4)}{5}.\n$$\n\nDividing by $5$ makes the rate a change per unit distance; using $\\mathbf v$ without normalizing would give a rate for a different speed of travel."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186725181 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "33eaab04-018f-5107-a9c0-dbc0a20f046c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{37}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186725171} {:answer/id #uuid "976614fc-197f-58d7-ac53-f6c702510abe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{37}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186725178} {:answer/id #uuid "c004eadb-5f81-51fb-9145-d3f50212bdc0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{17}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186725184} {:answer/id #uuid "c9ec7bad-f547-51b0-b1f6-308c48848d60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{26}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186725186} {:answer/id #uuid "d6dd4840-087b-5a66-8dd2-b3ab1fca4baa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-37" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186725188}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "33eaab04-018f-5107-a9c0-dbc0a20f046c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{37}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186725171} :answer-field/id #uuid "86f2f715-b50b-5f2f-b2f8-0d988a4f9a07" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186725177}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "b61087b2-1f01-52ce-8366-a77ea047d90d" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x,y)=2xy+e^{2x-y}$. What is the signed rate of change of $f$ at the point $(1,2)$ in the direction in which the function decreases most rapidly?" :question/worked-solution "First compute the gradient, using the chain rule for the exponential term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x(x,y)&=2y+2e^{2x-y},\\\\\nf_y(x,y)&=2x-e^{2x-y},\\\\\n\\nabla f(1,2)&=\\langle 4+2,2-1\\rangle=\\langle 6,1\\rangle.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe direction of steepest decrease is opposite to the gradient. The corresponding unit vector is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf u=-\\frac{\\nabla f(1,2)}{\\|\\nabla f(1,2)\\|}\n=\\frac{\\langle-6,-1\\rangle}{\\sqrt{37}}.\n$$\n\nThe rate in that direction is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nD_{\\mathbf u}f(1,2)&=\\langle6,1\\rangle\\cdot\\frac{\\langle-6,-1\\rangle}{\\sqrt{37}}\\\\\n&=\\frac{-36-1}{\\sqrt{37}}=-\\sqrt{37}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, $D_{\\mathbf u}f=\\|\\nabla f\\|\\cos\\theta$ is smallest when $\\theta=\\pi$. The magnitude of the decrease is $\\sqrt{37}$, but the signed rate of change is $-\\sqrt{37}$."}]}
